\par\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Launches Data Center 'Workforce Academy'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky and Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Forget about learning to code: \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  says it's time to pick up a wrench.\par \par \uc2 The company is launching a "workforce academy" to train Americans to build its data centers as skilled trade workers become a sought-after commodity. The five-week training program, in partnership with \uc2 CBRE\uc2  and the Associated Builders and Contractors, is free of charge and guarantees graduates a job at a Meta data-center construction site, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  is committing $115 million to the program this year and will pilot it in Louisiana, Ohio, Indiana and Texas. The company's largest data center, called Hyperion, is in Richland Parish, La. It will be "so large that it would cover a significant part of Manhattan," the company has said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  joins a growing crowd of companies that have realized there is a dearth of skilled workers in the U.S. and are trying to encourage people to enter the field. The growth of data centers spurred an uptick in demand for skilled workers, particularly electricians and HVAC technicians to lay the infrastructure for facilities that require extensive power and precise heating and cooling systems. According to estimates from Associated Builders and Contractors, the construction industry needs to add an estimated 349,000 net new workers this year to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  is already spending hundreds of billions of dollars to build out the infrastructure it thinks it needs to compete in the artificial-intelligence race. It recently laid off 8,000 white-collar employees in part to fund those efforts.\par \par \uc2 The company has lofty ambitions to create personal and business agents for its 3.5 billion daily active users and is building AI models to achieve those goals. It has started tracking employees' mouse clicks and keystrokes to train its AI models on how to use computers and has touted a future where AI agents primarily do the work and employees supervise them.\par \par \uc2 Finding the workers needed to help build its data centers has been an area of focus recently. In April, \uc2 Meta\uc2  unveiled a fiber-installation training program. The company says it received 35,000 applications for the program in its first seven days.\par \par \uc2 Big business and their philanthropic arms have been pouring money into shoring up the future pipeline of trade workers this year, including a $100 million initiative the BlackRock Foundation announced earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260609em690000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
