\par\par \b \uc2 Employers Push Back On Weight-Loss Drugs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ray A. Smith and Peter Loftus \par \uc2 999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Coverage for Ozempic and other drugs used for weight loss has fast become one of the most coveted workplace benefits -- so much so that companies say they can no longer afford to provide it.\par \par \uc2 With as many as 1 in 8 American adults taking the pills or injectables, big employers from \uc2 Cigna\uc2  to \uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2  are dropping coverage of so-called GLP-1s in droves. Others, like \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , are making workers jump through extra hoops to get coverage -- and to ensure the drugs are used effectively -- such as requiring multiple weigh-ins a month, meal-tracking on apps or sessions with an online health coach.\par \par \uc2 More than a quarter of big companies say they are adding criteria this year or next, while 11% have dropped or are planning to drop coverage for weight-loss purposes altogether, according to soon-to-be released data from benefits-advisory firm \uc2 Mercer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Globally, the portion of companies covering GLP-1s for weight loss has fallen to 23% this year from 30% in 2024, a new survey of more than 400 companies by the \uc2 International Foundation of Employee Benefit Plans\uc2  shows.\par \par \uc2 Under those circumstances, Samantha Stratton says she is just glad her employer's plan will still cover her Wegovy injections at all. She and her doctor lobbied to get her on the \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  drug last year after the plan stopped covering the rival Zepbound shots she had been taking. The approval, though, came with a catch: She must log her weight and meet with a nutritionist at least once a month via an app, something she didn't have to do when she was on Zepbound.\par \par \uc2 Still, "it's a small ask to avoid paying thousands out of pocket," says Stratton, who works in the work-life benefits industry and lives in West Haven, Conn. Plus, she thinks the structure has helped her stick to the regimen. She has lost 130 pounds since she began GLP-1 medications.\par \par \uc2 The drugs aren't just a cost concern for employers. They have also become important tools for attracting and retaining staff. In a recent survey of more than 1,000 workers by insurance broker \uc2 NFP\uc2 , about 30% said they would switch jobs if that got them coverage for GLP-1 medications.\par \par \uc2 That's a big reason many companies extended coverage of the popular drugs to weight-loss use -- and not just diabetes treatment -- in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Wooing workers, though, has become less of a priority as companies' spending on GLP-1 drugs has ballooned. \uc2 HCA Healthcare\uc2 , which employs more than 300,000 workers across its hospitals and medical centers, stopped covering the drugs for weight-loss use as of January after use of GLP-1s on its employee plan surged 90% in 2025, driving up costs. Like most companies, it still covers it for diabetes.\par \par \uc2 Blue Cross Blue Shield of Massachusetts\uc2  has stopped coverage of GLP-1s for obesity treatment in the plans it sells to employers, too. As of January, employers can instead buy the coverage as an add-on, and 20% have opted to, says Amy McHugh, a spokeswoman for the insurer.\par \par \uc2 Had it done nothing, the insurer projects its GLP-1 costs would run close to $1 billion this year, nearly double its costs in 2025 and seven times the $140 million it spent in 2023. That "would mean significantly higher premiums for everyone, whether they use these medications or not," McHugh adds.\par \par \uc2 "We certainly hear a lot of angst about this," says Jeff Levin-Scherz, a senior managing director in health and benefits at \uc2 WTW\uc2 , formerly known as \uc2 Willis Towers Watson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many of the benefits-consulting firm's clients are experiencing double-digit increases in medical and pharmacy costs, he says.\par \par \uc2 Some employers and health economists maintain that paying for GLP-1s will ultimately pay off if slimmed-down workers stick around long enough for the potential savings to kick in from, say, avoiding or reversing obesity-related joint or heart issues. A growing body of research suggests the drugs have even broader health benefits -- such as helping to prevent or stall cancer -- that could trigger additional savings.\par \par \uc2 Soren Bjorn, chief executive of fresh-berry supplier Driscoll's, said that calculus was a point of debate among executives at the Milken Institute Global Conference in Los Angeles in May. Those whose companies had leaned in to broader GLP-1 coverage for employees said that after 18 to 24 months, they had started to see overall healthcare costs come down, he said. "So for our employees next year, it's going to be free," he said recently at The Wall Street Journal's Global Food Forum.\par \par \uc2 Some research backs up the idea. Individuals who took a GLP-1 for 18 months saw slower medical-cost growth and a reduction in hospitalizations, according to a January study published by \uc2 Aon\uc2 , which looked at the claims of 192,000 GLP-1 users who started the drugs between November 2021 and March 2025. Other studies suggest current prices for the drugs -- ranging from a $149 cash price to a list price of $1,350 a month, depending on the type, dosage and discount options -- still aren't cost-effective enough.\par \par \uc2 The leading manufacturers of GLP-1s, Eli Lilly and Novo Nordisk, have taken steps to persuade more self-insured employers to cover the drugs. Lilly formed a team in 2023 to visit large employers and coalitions of employers that share tips on ways to control their healthcare spending.\par \par \uc2 Lilly also recently started a service that lets employers set up weight-loss drug coverage outside of traditional benefits. As part of it, Lilly sells Zepbound injection pens at a discounted price of $449 a month for all doses, and will soon start selling its weight-loss pill at $399 a month. Yet overall commercial insurance coverage of GLP-1s for weight loss has stalled at roughly 50%, Lilly and Novo say.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich446\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6690
