\par\par \b \uc2 Big Employers Spend to Fix Technician Gap\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 693 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Every morning, the chief executive of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  checks the same number: How big is the nationwide mechanic shortage at its dealerships? Lately it has hovered around 5,000.\par \par \uc2 It was even worse a couple of years ago, but this is still a yawning gap the automaker has to close, Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said. The technician shortage drives up labor costs and delays repairs.\par \par \uc2 "Customers are feeling the pain," Farley said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Big employers such as Ford and philanthropies are boosting programs to persuade more Americans to don tool belts. New efforts announced just this year already total around $400 million.\par \par \uc2 The latest campaign is a $90 million Bloomberg Philanthropies effort launching this week focused on helping more high-school students land jobs in the trades. The effort includes a partnership with Ford in Detroit, where \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  and the automaker are kicking in $2.5 million each, partly to fund new auto-repair bays for high-schoolers.\par \par \uc2 The goal is to train 300 auto mechanics over the next three years who will be eligible to start jobs directly after high school with Ford dealerships, boosting the pipeline of younger workers.\par \par \uc2 "Most of our technicians are older -- it is a real dilemma," Farley said.\par \par \uc2 This year alone, the automaker says it is spending $300 million on efforts aimed at filling vital jobs.\par \par \uc2 Overall, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  said its latest spending will support these sorts of efforts in more than half a dozen states, including classroom renovations and paid work stipends for student apprentices.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. skilled workforce is aging fast, while demand for workers in the trades multiplies quickly because of surging data-center demand and investment in advanced manufacturing. According to estimates from Associated Builders and Contractors, the construction industry needs to add an estimated 349,000 net new workers this year alone to meet demand, fueled in part by a skyrocketing need for electricians.\par \par \uc2 But there is a new generation of potential workers to tap: Members of Gen Z have shown growing interest in blue-collar careers, to the point where districts are revitalizing shop classes in some areas, and there is a rising tide of philanthropic spending aimed at aggressively accelerating that trend.\par \par \uc2 Lowe's Foundation, for example, has pledged $250 million to revitalize the trades, with most of that funding announced this spring. The goal is to train 250,000 people by 2035, including hiring more instructors and creating mobile classrooms in rural areas. In another effort, the retailer's foundation is also spending money on a three-part television series to highlight stories of workers pursuing careers in the skilled trades, as well as those of their mentors.\par \par \uc2 "Within our society, we've created a belief there's only one pathway to be successful, and that is you have to go to college, you have to get a four-year degree," said \uc2 Marvin Ellison\uc2 , Lowe's chief executive. "There are multiple pathways."\par \par \uc2 Students seeking a foothold in the trades often face wait lists or don't know how to navigate their options, said Claire Chamberlain, global head of social impact at the BlackRock Foundation, the money manager's philanthropic arm.\par \par \uc2 "There are interested workers, there are would-be workers, and jobs on the other side," she said. "It's the training systems that are not offering the capacity that industry demands."\par \par \uc2 To remedy that, \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  said earlier this year that it is spending $100 million to support the skilled trades, including efforts in Texas, where there is steep demand for electricians as data centers crop up there. The goal is to help train some 12,000 electricians over three years in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The Eastern Atlantic States Regional Council of Carpenters, a union, has agreed to set aside 75 apprenticeship slots for high-school graduates who are trained through the Bloomberg program. The union also plans to develop carpentry classwork in some high schools that don't yet have it, plus expand summer programs for high-schoolers that help expose them to the trade.\par \par \uc2 The council expects 60 participants to enroll in such boot camps this summer.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260608em6800003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
