\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Job Openings Rise While Hiring Falls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Coacci \par \uc2 216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. job openings increased while hiring fell in April, according to a report from the Labor Department's monthly job openings and labor turnover survey.\par \par \uc2 Available positions increased to 7.6 million in April, from 6.9 million in March. The rate of job openings rose to 4.6% in April, from 4.2% in March.\par \par \uc2 The rate of workers facing layoffs was little changed, at 1.1% in April compared with 1.2% in March.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the hiring rate worsened to 3.2% in April from 3.5% in March, with about 5.1 million Americans landing new jobs in April.\par \par \uc2 The quit rate -- workers leaving their jobs voluntarily -- was 1.9% in April compared with 2% in March. The rate can serve as a measure of workers' willingness or ability to leave their job.\par \par \uc2 April's job scenario reflected what Fed officials have described as relatively stable, with little signals of any harsh downturns as layoffs remain low. But the data may suggest continuing economic uncertainty weighed on firms' decisions to hire more workers.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, economists will get a better read on the labor market, as the employment report for May will be released by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6645
