\par\par \b \uc2 Auto Strike Hits Some Of GM's Bestsellers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hundreds of unionized workers walked off the line on Monday at an auto-parts supplier in Three Rivers, Mich. Their leverage is about 160 miles away, in Detroit: \uc2 General Motors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The work stoppage at the \uc2 Dauch Corp.\uc2  plant -- still commonly known by its former name, \uc2 American Axle\uc2  -- halts the supply of axles and axle tubes destined for GM's midsize and full-size pickups. If GM's existing stockpile of the crucial parts runs out, the automaker won't be able to make some of its bestselling and most lucrative vehicles. The situation imperils GM's heavy-duty Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra pickups, as well as midsize trucks that compete for buyers with \uc2 Toyota\uc2 's Tacoma and Ford's Ranger.\par \par \uc2 "The loss of a key supplier such as \uc2 American Axle\uc2  could slow down output of GM's trucks," said \uc2 Sam Fiorani\uc2 , vice president at consulting firm AutoForecast Solutions. He added that because auto plants consume parts as soon as they arrive, "production issues will arise rather quickly."\par \par \uc2 For now, GM's truck assembly plants are running as normal, and the company said it is monitoring the situation. Josh Jager, a veteran \uc2 American Axle\uc2  worker who is leading the negotiations for the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , said it appears GM has about two weeks' worth of axles in stock and no alternate supplier for them.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 American Axle\uc2  is trying to keep the Michigan plant running with a "skeleton crew" of nonunion contractors, Jager said.\par \par \uc2 Detroit-based \uc2 Dauch Corp.\uc2 , which changed its name from \uc2 American Axle and Manufacturing Holdings\uc2  upon merging with a European auto supplier this year, called the workers' decision to strike "disappointing."\par \par \uc2 "We remain committed to negotiating with the union in good faith and hope to promptly reach a fair agreement," \uc2 Dauch Corp.\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The work stoppage occurred as the \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract with the plant expired at midnight Monday without a new deal in place.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the plant say they are long overdue for significant raises after swallowing a roughly 50% pay cut in 2008 to keep the plant running. "We did what we had to do to stay open," said Jager, who started at the Three Rivers plant in 2002.\par \par \uc2 Regular line workers still don't earn the $29 an hour the plant paid in the mid-2000s, even before adjusting for inflation, they say. Their goal is to raise wages to $30.50 an hour, up from the current $22, along with more generous health coverage and more vacation time, Jager said.\par \par \uc2 The spat with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is as much about GM as it is about \uc2 American Axle\uc2 , as the supplier will likely try to pass much of the cost of increased wages for union workers to its main customer, said Warren Browne, an independent consultant to auto suppliers.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is telling GM, we are going to take aim at some of your most profitable vehicles," Browne said. "They're probably more people sweating at GM today than there are at \uc2 American Axle\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Axles are the long, steel rods that connect the wheels and help support the weight of a truck. They are large, high-value parts with dozens of components, including ring gears and pinions, specifically made for GM's trucks -- and thus not easily substituted.\par \par \uc2 If GM wanted to relocate the \uc2 American Axle\uc2  factory's equipment and restart production elsewhere, the process would take months, Browne said.\par \par \uc2 One auto industry analyst, Tom Narayan of \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , predicted that the strike may not last long, due to the plant's importance to GM. "GM, we expect, will step in to ensure production resumes," he said in a note to clients Monday. "We also expect there is adequate inventory to absorb lost production for a certain period."\par \par \uc2 American Axle\uc2 's ties to GM run deep. The Three Rivers facility and four other driveline and forging plants in Michigan and New York were once GM-owned facilities. In the 1990s, \uc2 Richard E. Dauch\uc2 , a former Chrysler executive, put together an investment group to buy them, forming an independent company that supplies parts to GM and other automakers. Dauch is now one of the largest automotive suppliers in North America.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 American Axle\uc2  last endured a strike in 2008, 3,650 workers from five plants in Michigan and New York also walked off the line for 87 days. The move shut down or curtailed operations at about 30 GM plants. Back then, \uc2 American Axle\uc2  was scrambling to cut costs and hunker down as its main customer, GM, teetered toward bankruptcy the following year.\par \par \uc2 Some of the same workers are taking part in the current strike, but say that it is happening under very different circumstances.\par \par \uc2 This time, the strike involves only the Three Rivers plant and its 970 hourly workers, as the former \uc2 American Axle\uc2  plants no longer have the combined leverage of operating under a single labor contract.\par \par \uc2 Today, both GM and Dauch are solidly profitable. GM has said its heavy-duty trucks remain in high demand, even as gas prices rise; the plant that builds them is moving from five days of production a week to six.\par \par \uc2 Mark Hicks won't be buying one, however. The longtime American Axle worker last bought a new truck -- a GMC Sierra -- in 2007, when he made about $29 per hour, he said.\par \par \uc2 After the 2008 agreement, his wage fell to $18. It has since climbed back to $22.\par \par \uc2 "I've had to cut back on a lot of things," Hicks, 67 years old, told The Wall Street Journal. "I can't even afford the product I'm building."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich386\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5790
