\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 On the Clock: A New Career Odyssey Awaits Today's College Graduates --- As the nature of work changes, job seekers are encouraged to develop transferable skills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 1,024 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The young people crossing commencement stages this spring are launching careers that will change in ways they can't imagine.\par \par \uc2 Some jobs they take now won't exist in the future. Positions they hold tomorrow may be inconceivable today.\par \par \uc2 Martian data-center engineer? That'll be a real job someday if \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  have anything to say about it. Presumably Musk's dreamed-of colony on Mars would have other job opportunities, too.\par \par \uc2 College graduates have never been guaranteed predictability, but the current cohort is notable for the degree of disruption it is likely to face. \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  projects people entering the workforce now will have twice as many jobs over the course of their careers as those who started out 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 This is partly because careers keep stretching longer. You're bound to experience more change if you work for 50 or 60 years than if you retire after a 40-year run.\par \par \uc2 It's also more common to work a job that is disconnected from your undergraduate major. About 42% of recent college grads, ages 22 to 27, have jobs that don't require degrees at all, never mind ones in the fields they studied. This is up from about 38% in early 2023, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, of course, there is the unknowable impact of artificial intelligence. I've argued that AI's role in recent job losses is overblown, but it is hard to imagine there won't be seismic effects at some point in Gen Zers' careers.\par \par \uc2 Older generations will feel those, too. Anyone planning to work for another decade or longer could be in for a bumpy ride to retirement.\par \par \uc2 Still, the phrase "ignorance is bliss" applies here. Most of us didn't know AI was coming when we began our careers, so we didn't worry about it. Today's grads, with their entire professional lives before them, understand they are on the precipice of something big and carry the anxiety that comes with this knowledge.\par \par \uc2 Their task is to develop transferable skills to thrive in a working world that may turn out to be unrecognizable.\par \par \uc2 This is a daunting mission, but experienced pivoters have some tips.\par \par \uc2 Jim Jaffe, 90 years old, is in the consulting phase of a career that started in his father's microfilm business and included a private-equity stint in the middle. He advises clients, mostly entrepreneurs, to produce one-sheets that capture the essence of their businesses. Making a personal one-sheet is a good idea too, he says.\par \par \uc2 Jaffe showed me his. It highlights fundamental skills like inventory management and lead generation, alongside his experience working overseas. For yourself as much as any external audience, he contends, it is important to identify strengths that aren't tied to certain jobs or industries.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Iselin\uc2  studied photography and art history at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Now she is chief executive of health-insurance company \uc2 Blue Cross Blue Shield of Massachusetts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An artist's eye for detail served her well when she moved into healthcare. And who better to distill reams of complex information than someone who understands a picture is worth a thousand words?\par \par \uc2 To be clear, subject-matter knowledge is important. Iselin went back to school for a graduate degree in health policy and management. But she credits art with training her mind to see and communicate clearly.\par \par \uc2 Just as important as recognizing interdisciplinary connections for herself was building a network of people who would vouch for her.\par \par \uc2 "Early in our careers we don't have a deep appreciation of how small the world really is," Iselin says.\par \par \uc2 People talk. So if you're gunning for a job that is unlike what you studied or did in the past, you need references who will advocate taking a chance on you.\par \par \uc2 Some colleges are tracking down alumni with unconventional career paths like Iselin's and presenting them as role models. It's a twist on alumni networking, which traditionally involves students meeting grads who work in their fields of study, hoping connections will lead to jobs or internships.\par \par \uc2 The new approach is about demonstrating adaptability.\par \par \uc2 "We want to show students our alumni journeys," says Joe Catrino, executive director of the Center for Career Design at \uc2 Dartmouth College\uc2 . "I'm foundationally opposed to bridging major to career."\par \par \uc2 Catrino's office was previously called the Center for Professional Development. The rebrand signals an effort to help young people design multichapter careers around raw abilities, rather than develop narrow skills for specific professions.\par \par \uc2 Some of those abilities -- communication, critical thinking -- are oft-repeated to the point of triteness. Catrino cites one that stands out: reflection. Students returning from internships debrief with coaches. Every session of a class he teaches begins with journaling.\par \par \uc2 Those of us already in the workforce would be wise to practice reflection because it will surely be baked into interview questions for future jobs.\par \par \uc2 What did you learn in previous roles that will enable you to succeed in this newly created position?\par \par \uc2 We better come ready with thoughtful answers.\par \par \uc2 One worthwhile exercise is to fill out the "soft skills" and "technical skills" sections of your LinkedIn profile. Kory Kantenga, LinkedIn's head of economics for the Americas, tells me recruiters increasingly filter job candidates by the skills listed in their bios. This is because titles become less relevant as the nature of work changes.\par \par \uc2 Someone looking to hire a Martian data-center engineer -- or fill any job that previously didn't exist -- won't find applicants with direct experience.\par \par \uc2 "Employers are going to have to imagine what roles people can do," Kantenga says.\par \par \uc2 Let's not rely only on their imaginations, though. If we want to give ourselves the best chance to remain employable in the distant future, it will be incumbent on us to help companies see our potential for jobs we've never done before.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260603em6300037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
