\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Strike Set at Key GM Supplier's Plant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 593 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 June 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 1,000 workers at a Michigan automotive supplier were set to strike, stalling the production of key parts for \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ' midsize and full-size pickup trucks.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 American Axle\uc2  plant in Three Rivers, Mich., are at an impasse with the company over a new labor contract. Workers say their pay hasn't recovered from 2008, when they agreed to slash wages in half to keep the plant running during dire economic times, despite high profit at \uc2 American Axle\uc2  in recent years on the strength of GM's truck sales.\par \par \uc2 "For 18 years, these members have built you an empire of profit, while getting treated like dirt. They've taken wage cuts, benefit cuts. They've poured their souls into this plant," said \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain in a livestream announcing the strike late Sunday. The work stoppage was to start Monday at 12:01 a.m.\par \par \uc2 "We'll stay out on strike until this company comes to its senses and treats these workers with dignity and respect," Fain added.\par \par \uc2 American Axle\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Axle\uc2  plant produces axles for GM's heavy-duty Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra pickups, as well as for its midsize trucks, the Chevrolet Colorado and GMC Canyon. The plant also makes axle tubes that ultimately end up in light-duty Silverados and Sierras.\par \par \uc2 While GM is its main customer, the plant also makes components for another automotive supplier and for Chrysler's Pacifica minivan.\par \par \uc2 The strike comes at an inopportune time for GM, which is trying to take advantage of its rival \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 's limited pickup production due to an aluminum shortage. It is also fending off renewed competition from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , whose Ram truck sales are up 23% in 2026 from a year earlier, according to Motor Intelligence. GM's trucks are among its most profitable models.\par \par \uc2 GM is trying to increase production of its heavy-duty Silverados and Sierras just as the workers who make the axles for those trucks are going on strike. GM's Flint, Mich., plant, which assembles the pickups, is set to add a sixth day of weekly production in June.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Axle\uc2  plant also supplies GM's Oshawa, Ontario, truck plant, which assembles heavy-duty Silverados in addition to those made in Flint.\par \par \uc2 Josh Jager, a 24-year \uc2 American Axle\uc2  employee and chairman of the bargaining committee for UAW Local 2093, accused the company of stockpiling axles since January in anticipation of a labor dispute as its union contract expired Sunday. GM appears to have about two weeks of axles in stock, he said.\par \par \uc2 Jager, like other plant employees, saw his wage slashed from $29 per hour to $14.50 in 2008, when workers feared the plant would close. Today, most production workers at the plant top out at $22 an hour, he said. He said he would be making about $25 an hour, had he not moved into a higher-paying skilled trades role.\par \par \uc2 "We have members that take out a loan on their 401(k) almost every 12 to 24 months to pay off credit card debt, and it's just a perpetual thing because they can't make enough money," Jager said. "A guy works seven days a week, $22 an hour, and he can't support himself."\par \par \uc2 Besides more money, the workers are seeking limits to \uc2 American Axle\uc2 's ability to compel them into overtime production on weekends.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260601em610006e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
