\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- Why the Dream of the Feel-Good Millennial Brand Didn't Last --- For a generation of shoppers, Everlane's 'ethical' approach heralded an era of mindful consumerism. Then the vibes shifted and reality set in.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Schube \par \uc2 947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The clothing brand Everlane burst onto the fashion scene in 2011 with a bold proclamation: "The finest designer-quality accessories, basics & essentials under $100." Over time, the promise of fair pricing became enmeshed with an even loftier assurance: By partnering with "ethical factories," it would sell better-made clothing in a way that didn't run afoul of the customer's morals.\par \par \uc2 For a while, it worked -- very well. The brand's boxy tees and crew neck sweaters became omnipresent on creative-class millennials in cities like New York and San Francisco. After initially intending to remain fully online, Everlane opened a physical store in New York in 2017, and later added 10 additional locations around the country. By 2016, it was looking to raise money at a valuation of $250 million, according to Recode.\par \par \uc2 Everlane boomed alongside a cohort of like-minded brands, including \uc2 Warby Parker\uc2  (eyeglasses), Casper (mattresses) and \uc2 Allbirds\uc2  (washable wool sneakers), that offered customers a shopping experience that flattered both their taste and their morals. They sold the right things in the right way.\par \par \uc2 So, customers were likely taken aback when earlier this month reports emerged that the company had been acquired by \uc2 Shein\uc2 , the Chinese fast-fashion brand whose runaway success has been tarnished by criticism of its labor practices and its negative environmental footprint.\par \par \uc2 Everlane confirmed news of the sale on May 22. "Everlane will remain an independent brand, staying true to our longstanding brand values, sustainability commitments, and exceptional quality," CEO \uc2 Alfred Chang\uc2  said in a statement. A representative for \uc2 Shein\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 "The juxtaposition of Everlane's initial positioning with the reputation of the business that is now acquiring them, it feels like satire," said Emily Heyward, co-founder and CEO of the branding agency Red Antler, which worked with many of the companies that defined this new wave of commerce in the 2010s. "It's hard not to see this as, like, the final death rattle of an era."\par \par \uc2 That era was defined by a distinctly of-its-time approach to marketing and selling products -- an approach that, at least initially, felt fresh.\par \par \uc2 Instead of selling a suite of diverse products, millennial brands focused on the "only" T-shirt, mattress or saute pan you'll ever need. They marketed their wares on social media with witty, socially progressive campaigns. And they raised enormous piles of cash -- sometimes in the hundreds of millions of dollars -- in a way that made them seem more like tech startups than companies hawking retail consumer goods.\par \par \uc2 And from a certain vantage point, the arc of history seemed to be bending toward social and political progress. A Black president occupied the Oval Office; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  had proven essential to pro-democracy movements in the Middle East. Who was to say online brands couldn't make the world a brighter, more tasteful place?\par \par \uc2 "It was a very idealistic era," Heyward said. "People were excited to engage with this new wave of businesses that seemed to be offering them both more value and more delight. Like, why do we have to go spend our Saturday at the mattress store? It sucks. And why can't our mattress brand be fun and funny?" (Casper was a client of Heyward's company.)\par \par \uc2 Everlane's clothes were chic but simple, tasteful without being ostentatious -- clothing for people who took themselves seriously. And while some chuckled when Everlane and its peers boasted about being tech-savvy, millennial brands did operate differently from their predecessors.\par \par \uc2 Startups like Everlane "were using technology in a way that most brands were not. They were communicating in a very direct way to people, and being very human in a way that was new," notes Dan Frommer, founder of the publication The New Consumer. Today's table stakes -- like a website you can scroll on a smartphone -- were then market-setting advantages.\par \par \uc2 But eventually, those advantages met with headwinds. Apple's emphasis on digital privacy made targeted marketing more difficult. Although physical locations helped reach more consumers, shifting to in-person retail meant abandoning the "just one great thing" model for enough products to fill an entire store -- a far cry from the "frictionless" experience of shopping online.\par \par \uc2 "If you can't do it profitably, if you don't have the right customer economics, all the vibes in the world are not going to save you," Frommer said.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, the vibes changed. Obama gave way to Trump; \uc2 TikTok\uc2  outmuscled \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 ; and fast-fashion giants like \uc2 Shein\uc2  and Temu rose up and grabbed significant market share. At the same time, influencers became the dominant force in marketing, more so than brands, and the appeal of ethical consumerism ran headlong into an economy battered by the pandemic and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Everlane was not immune. Yearly credit- and debit-card spending on the brand has lagged in every quarter but one since 2022, according to estimates from research firm Consumer Edge. In 2019, the company raised $90 million in debt financing.\par \par \uc2 "This wave of consumerism that was emerging in the 2010s could have carried the tide for the Everlanes if it had been sustained. But it got rocked a little bit in 2016, and now it's absolutely dead in the water," said Mark A. Cohen, the retired director of retail studies at Columbia Business School.\par \par \uc2 "In 2010, people would say, Yeah, I care about landfill. And in 2026, it's like, I've got bigger problems than that."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260530em5u00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
