\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: College Grads Have an Edge in Tight Job Market --- Outlook is still tough, but those without a bachelor's degree climb a higher hill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's a tough job market for young college graduates. For young people without a bachelor's degree, it's even tougher.\par \par \uc2 The college class of 2026 is entering one of the most nerve-racking job markets in recent memory. Employers have sharply reduced hiring -- awful news for those trying to land their first job. Worries that artificial intelligence will be able to perform many of the skills graduates spent years honing are running high. Commencement speakers lauding the technology are drawing jeers.\par \par \uc2 A widely followed quarterly report from the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 , based on the detailed data that underlies the Labor Department's monthly jobs report, shows that as of March, the unemployment rate for people with bachelor's degrees, and between the ages of 22 and 27, was a seasonally adjusted 5.6%. That was a big step up from the 3.6% registered in December 2019, before the Covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for all workers in that age group -- college graduates, those with associate degrees, those with just high-school degrees, and so on -- was higher at 7.2%. But the move higher from right before the pandemic, when it was 6.8%, was much smaller. So in relative terms, it looks as if the employment prospects for young college grads versus other young people have worsened significantly.\par \par \uc2 But unemployment rates are a narrow measure, and using them to draw comparisons between different groups of workers can be problematic, said Brad Hershbein, senior economist at the W.E. Upjohn Institute for Employment Research. His analysis of the same government data suggests that, if anything, the employment outlook for less-educated young people has weakened more than it has for college graduates.\par \par \uc2 "Young people with college degrees are more likely to be employed, they are more likely to be earning money," said Hershbein.\par \par \uc2 Among young people who don't have bachelor's degrees, there is a larger share who are sitting out -- meaning, not even looking for work. The Labor Department doesn't count them in the unemployment rate. Rather, the unemployment rate includes only people actively looking for work.\par \par \uc2 Hershbein found that in the first quarter, the labor-force participation rate -- the share of the population either working or looking for work -- averaged 75.9% for people whose educations span high school through associate degrees. That was down from 77.1% in the first quarter of 2019. Meanwhile, the participation rate for young people with bachelor's degrees was 86.7%, up from 85.7%.\par \par \uc2 So it looks as if the narrowing gap in the unemployment rate for young college graduates and their less-educated peers has come about because the college crowd is participating more actively in the labor market while others are moving to the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 A still more comprehensive measure is looking at the share of the population that is working. For college graduates ages 22 to 27, the employment-to-population ratio averaged 82.4% in the first quarter, according to Hershbein's analysis, nearly flat with its average of 82.5% in the first quarter of 2019. Meanwhile, for the group with high school through associate degrees, the employment-to-population ratio was 70.5%, down from 71.8%.\par \par \uc2 "If you look at anybody who has a diploma, but does not have a B.A., their outcome has gotten worse over the past few years," said Guy Berger, a labor economist who has run similar analyses.\par \par \uc2 Berger also emphasizes, however, that today's college graduates do face a difficult environment. Their employment outlook hasn't deteriorated as badly as it has for the less educated. But historically they have done better in the face of a weakening job market than they are doing now.\par \par \uc2 Part of the problem might be AI. But the bigger problem could be that they are competing in a workforce where having a bachelor's degree isn't nearly as special as it once was.\par \par \uc2 In a recent analysis of the detailed government data, economists at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  noted that the wage boost that workers with a bachelor's degree receive relative to less-educated workers, while still substantial, has slipped, falling to about 55% last year versus about 63% in 2015. That could be because the number of workers with college degrees keeps rising. As of March, about 42% of U.S. employees had a bachelor's degree or higher, versus about 36% a decade earlier.\par \par \uc2 It is a dynamic that has happened before. As \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  economists Claudia Goldin and Lawrence Katz have documented, in the early 1900s only a small fraction of workers had high-school degrees, and having one earned workers a substantial wage premium. But that premium was eaten away as having a high-school degree went from rare, to common, to almost universal.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich486\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7290
