\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Standard Charter CEO Apologizes For AI in the Workplace Comment\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Wallace \par \uc2 459 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered CEO \uc2 Bill Winters\uc2  struggled to draw a line under comments that the bank would replace "lower-value human capital" with AI.\par \par \uc2 His attempt to contextualize his comments in a \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  post Friday backfired. Three hours later, he posted an apology.\par \par \uc2 It is an example of the difficulties CEOs face in describing AI in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Winters tried to douse the furor with an initial post on Friday that said \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  "will continue to speak honestly about the impact of technological change." He suggested he had been misunderstood, saying his point had been that employers have a responsibility to move workers from jobs that are more vulnerable to automation into "higher-value roles."\par \par \uc2 Soon he was back with an apology. "I have received a lot of support for the messages in my previous post but still get questions about my choice of words, which I know has caused upset to some colleagues. For that I am sorry," Winters said, appending a partial transcript of the meeting in which he made the comment.\par \par \uc2 The kerfuffle began Tuesday, when Winters was in Hong Kong to announce goals, which included a plan to reduce by 15% the lender's workforce of back-office jobs by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to a group of journalists, he used the example of a tech upgrade in Hong Kong that killed some jobs, and said the aim of automation wasn't to cut costs. "It is replacing in some cases lower-value human capital with the financial capital and the investment capital we're putting in," he said.\par \par \uc2 London-based Standard Chartered specializes in emerging-markets lending, with a heavy focus on Asia and the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Winters tried to walk back the comments Wednesday with a memo to employees in which he said "where roles do fall away, it reflects changes in the work, not the value of our people."\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  boss \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  attempted to defend Winters, a former protege. "Bill's a friend of mine and all of us say something incorrectly," he told \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 . "But I also think it will be all jobs. I don't think it will be higher level, lower level. I think it will be more than you think."\par \par \uc2 Winters was on a trip to Hong Kong with \uc2 Georges Elhedery\uc2 , chief executive of rival bank \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Speaking to his own bank's investors after Winters, Elhedery was more cautious.\par \par \uc2 "AI will destroy certain jobs and will create new jobs," he said. "But I need 200,000 colleagues with us on this journey." Elhedery said the challenge was to make all employees feel empowered by AI so they don't resist it.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260523em5n0005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
