\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: New York's Unions Post Big Wins Amid Region's Affordability Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Louise Ensign, Kate King and Justin Lahart \par \uc2 1,100 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New York City's unionized workers are getting big raises.\par \par \uc2 Hotel housekeepers won a contract this week that will have them earning six figures in coming years. Striking commuter train workers, who earned more than $135,000 on average, won pay increases this week too. The city's doormen and nurses notched similar gains earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The deals show the power of organized labor in a city where concerns about the cost of living are widespread and public officials are sympathetic to unions and their message. Union officials say the wage increases are necessary for their workers, who provide essential services that keep the city running, to live comfortably in an expensive metropolitan area.\par \par \uc2 Business owners say the wage increases will raise prices for consumers, with higher hotel bills and healthcare costs. In negotiations settled Monday, the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2  argued that the wage increases that \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  unions were asking for would lead to higher fares or increased borrowing.\par \par \uc2 Labor economists and union supporters said union victories in New York City could be hard to replicate elsewhere, but across the country unions have been flexing a bit more muscle in recent years. And other workers, struggling to keep up with rising costs, could take notice.\par \par \uc2 Strike activity across the country, though still low, has picked up the past several years, Labor Department figures show. And while only 10% of U.S. workers are in unions, compared with about a third in the mid-1950s, American attitudes toward organized labor have become friendlier. Last year, 68% of people polled by Gallup said they were in favor of unions, up from a low of 48% logged in 2009.\par \par \uc2 New York City's mayor, \uc2 Zohran Mamdani\uc2 , won on a platform of championing working-class New Yorkers and taxing the wealthy, who have reacted with alarm. Mamdani walked the picket lines with nurses and addressed a rally of union workers on May Day. "We have to make it easier to raise a family in New York City," Mamdani said in a video supporting doormen.\par \par \uc2 Unionized apartment-building workers, including doormen, are reviewing a tentative agreement that would increase their hourly wages $4.50 an hour by 2030, according to the union representing them.\par \par \uc2 If ratified, union members would also maintain free healthcare benefits and receive a 15% guaranteed pension increase, the union said. Employees would earn at least $71,000 in annual wages by the end of the four-year contract.\par \par \uc2 Nurses at city hospitals also held their largest and longest strike in city history this year, securing salary increases of more than 12% over the course of the three-year contract.\par \par \uc2 Flandersia Jones, 62, lives in the Bronx and has worked as a nurse for more than 40 years. She received a roughly $100 boost to her biweekly paycheck working in oncology at BronxCare earlier this year. But it doesn't feel like nearly enough.\par \par \uc2 Rent on her two-bedroom apartment is nearly $3,000 a month. Groceries are costly, and the price of gas for her car is up markedly this year. She earns about $140,000 at her job but picks up additional work to support her family.\par \par \uc2 "Things are very expensive, extraordinarily expensive," said Jones, who is a board member of her union.\par \par \uc2 The city's Hotel and Gaming Trades Council on Monday said it won the union's largest pay increase in its nearly 100-year history for 30,000 members working at nearly 250 hotels. Under the new contract, which will take effect July 1 if ratified by union members, the hourly wage for most workers will increase 50%.\par \par \uc2 A housekeeper's hourly wage will increase from $39.87 to $61.07 by the final year of the eight-year contract, according to the union. Housekeepers will be earning more than $100,000 annually by the sixth year of the contract.\par \par \uc2 The new contract maintains free healthcare for union members and their families. Employers will also contribute to newly created funds that will help workers with housing and child-care costs, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 The hotel union is one of the most powerful groups in New York politics. Union President Rich Maroko set the stage for the recent contract win by advocating for legislation, signed last year by New York Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, that increased the maximum amount of unemployment insurance residents could collect 72%, to $869 a week.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest leverage Maroko wielded in contract negotiations was the threat of potential New York City legislation that would have reduced the amount of square footage hotel housekeepers would be required to clean before collecting double pay.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles previously passed a series of minimum-wage increases for airport and hotel workers that would have lifted hourly pay to at least $30 by mid-2028, just in time for the Olympic Games. On Tuesday, the L.A. city council voted 11-4 to delay implementation of the wage increase to 2030.\par \par \uc2 Vijay Dandapani\uc2 , chief executive of the Hotel Association of New York City, which represents hotel owners, said the housekeeping bill would have increased labor costs by 40%. "That would have been crushing," he said.\par \par \uc2 Maroko said members were motivated to fight for wage increases by the high cost of living.\par \par \uc2 "Over 80% of our members are the primary wage earners in their families," he said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Tracking New York's Cost of Living\par \par \uc2 The cost of living in New York City has soared in recent decades, propelled in part by the lofty earnings of finance workers.\par \par \uc2 In their world, a sit-down lunch at a Midtown Manhattan restaurant can easily top $100, an after-work drink can run $30, and tuition for many private schools is nearly $70,000 a year -- per child.\par \par \uc2 While their spending can fuel the local economy, it also can push up prices for everyone else.\par \par \uc2 Even families who would be considered well-off elsewhere are struggling to cover necessities such as child care and housing in the city, and the run of inflation following the pandemic has made things worse.\par \par \uc2 The average cost of sending a child to a daycare center is $26,000 a year, up 43% since 2019, according to a 2025 report from the city's comptroller.\par \par \uc2 The median asking rent in New York rose 7% from a year earlier in April to a record $4,120 a month, according to StreetEasy, a real-estate app owned by Zillow.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich307\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4605
