\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Intuit\uc2  to Cut Staff, Invest in 'Big Bets'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal and Kristin Broughton \par \uc2 479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Intuit\uc2  plans to lay off 17% of its workforce and invest the savings in "big bets" as it makes artificial intelligence a centerpiece of its business.\par \par \uc2 The maker of TurboTax and QuickBooks said Wednesday that slimming down its staff would improve efficiency companywide. The restructuring is expected to cost about $300 million to $340 million, most of which will be recognized in the current summer quarter.\par \par \uc2 Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Sandeep Aujla\uc2  said the layoffs are focused on coordination-related roles including project managers and business operations teams. Mid- and lower-level managerial roles are being removed as well.\par \par \uc2 The resulting company will be leaner and more focused, according to Aujla, who said the cuts weren't directly tied to \uc2 Intuit\uc2 's own use of AI.\par \par \uc2 "We've just got to make sure that more folks are at the forefront of dealing with the customers -- and particularly in this age, it's around managing agents as opposed to you know managing a set of people," Aujla said.\par \par \uc2 Intuit\uc2  didn't provide a projection of expected long-term savings from the layoffs. Those savings will be used to invest in the company's "big bets" and otherwise flow toward improving margins, Aujla said.\par \par \uc2 The Mountain View, Calif., company long known for its tax-preparation software has been remaking itself as an AI-first platform. It partnered with \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  earlier this year to build AI agents for its consumer and business clients, and teamed with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  last year to deepen its use of generative AI models.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts were announced in tandem with the results from \uc2 Intuit\uc2 's critical tax-season quarter, which ran from February through April 30.\par \par \uc2 Revenue climbed 10% to $8.56 billion, just ahead of analyst targets, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Consumer revenue was up 8% at $5.3 billion, including a 7% jump for TurboTax and 15% growth at \uc2 Credit Karma\uc2 . Its business-focused verticals recorded even bigger gains.\par \par \uc2 The company posted a profit of $3.06 billion, or $11.09 a share, compared with $2.82 billion, or $10.02 a share, in the same quarter a year earlier. Stripping out one-time items, adjusted earnings were $12.80 a share, beating analyst projections for $12.57.\par \par \uc2 For the full fiscal year, which ends July 31, \uc2 Intuit\uc2  raised its revenue growth expectations for both the business and consumer segments. Total revenue is expected to rise 11% to 12% for the final three months of the current fiscal year, with fourth-quarter adjusted earnings expected to hit $3.56 to $3.62 a share. Analysts polled by FactSet had been projecting $3.14 a share.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Intuit\uc2  fell more than 10% in after-hours trading following the results. Through Wednesday's close, the stock has dropped more than 40% this year.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260521em5l0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
