\par\par \b \uc2 CEO Walks Back Comment About AI Replacing 'Lower-Value' Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Wallace \par \uc2 363 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered Chief Executive \uc2 Bill Winters\uc2  touched a nerve when he said his bank would slash thousands of jobs and replace "lower-value human capital" with artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 He walked back the comments on Wednesday in a memo to bank employees, who turned out to be valuable enough that he needed to assuage their feelings.\par \par \uc2 "Many of you will have seen media coverage following the investor event in Hong Kong, particularly the reporting around automation, AI, and workforce changes," Winters said in the memo. "I know this may be unsettling when reduced to simple headlines or a quote out of context."\par \par \uc2 He added: "Where roles do fall away, it reflects changes in the work, not the value of our people."\par \par \uc2 Winters, a \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  veteran who has run London-listed \uc2 StanChart\uc2  since 2015, has a reputation for speaking bluntly. His latest headline-grabbing comments came in Hong Kong, where he was presenting new financial targets to investors including a plan to reduce the bank's support staff by more than 15% by 2030, equating to 7,800 jobs or more.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to journalists before the presentation on Tuesday, Winters said: "It's not cost cutting. It's replacing in some cases lower-value human capital with the financial capital and the investment capital we're putting in."\par \par \uc2 The comments drew criticism on social media and by former Singaporean president \uc2 Halimah Yacob\uc2 . "It's disturbing to read workers described as 'lower-value human capital'," she wrote on her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 The reaction speaks to growing backlash against AI. White-collar workers worry about their job prospects as the technology's capabilities rapidly improve, and in the U.S., public opinion about AI is quickly souring.\par \par \uc2 StanChart\uc2  had roughly 81,000 employees at the end of 2025 and a further 17,000 contract workers. On the firing line are roles in functions including human resources and risk and compliance.\par \par \uc2 Under Winters, \uc2 StanChart\uc2  has focused on handling cross-border transactions and banking affluent customers in Asia and the Middle East. For years, the bank's shares lagged behind those of larger rival \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260521em5l00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
