\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Begins Laying Off Thousands of Employees --- Cuts of roughly 8,000 jobs, or 10% of staff, are meant to offset AI costs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky and Raffaele Huang \par \uc2 531 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  began laying off thousands of employees Wednesday morning and reassigning thousands of others to artificial-intelligence-focused roles, according to an internal memo and people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief people officer, \uc2 Janelle Gale\uc2 , told staff last month that the coming layoffs would affect 10% of the company, or roughly 8,000 employees, and the company would cancel plans to hire for 6,000 open roles. In a follow-up memo Monday, she said it would move a separate 7,000 staffers into new AI-focused roles and transition a number of managers to individual-contributor roles as part of the reorganization.\par \par \uc2 Individual employees in Asia and Europe began receiving notifications that they would be affected early in their mornings, followed hours later by employees in the Americas, the people said.\par \par \uc2 In a memo to staff Wednesday morning, \uc2 Meta\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  thanked laid off employees for their contributions and called AI "the most consequential technology of our lifetimes."\par \par \uc2 "This is the most dynamic I have seen our industry. I'm optimistic about everything we're building," he said. "But success is not a given."\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  is full steam ahead into a gargantuan effort to reimagine its workforce and become more nimble to compete with AI-native startups. The company flattened teams and started tracking employees' keystrokes and mouse clicks to help train its AI models on how to use computers.\par \par \uc2 Executives said the job cuts are meant to offset \uc2 Meta\uc2 's increasing spending on AI infrastructure. The company plans up to $145 billion in capital expenditures this year, largely to build out AI data centers and fill them with chips, as it seeks to create what is called personal superintelligence for its 3.5 billion daily users.\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with employees a few weeks ago, Gale didn't rule out the possibility of future layoffs beyond this week's. On a call with analysts the same week, \uc2 Susan Li\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's finance chief, said she wasn't sure what the optimal size of the company would be in the future, citing "AI capabilities advancing rapidly."\par \par \uc2 "If a team used to take 50 or 100 people and now it takes 10, having 50 or 100 people on that team can actually be counterproductive going forward, so I think we need to fix that," Zuckerberg said during a recent meeting with employees.\par \par \uc2 In his Wednesday memo, the CEO said he didn't expect other companywide layoffs this year.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment among the company's staffers is at its most negative level on record, according to an analysis of posts by the anonymous workplace site Blind. More than 1,500 employees signed a petition demanding \uc2 Meta\uc2  not collect employee "computer-use" data to train its AI models.\par \par \uc2 When asked during its announcement if there was a way to opt out of the AI computer-tracking program, a \uc2 Meta\uc2  executive replied that there wasn't.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , owner of The Wall Street Journal, has a content-licensing partnership with \uc2 Meta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260521em5l0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
