\par\par \b \uc2 Lawsuit to Probe Sabbath Dispute\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Weber \par \uc2 333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  is famous among fans for closing on Sundays out of respect for the Christian sabbath. Now the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is suing one of its franchisees, alleging it denied an employee's request for Saturdays off to observe her sabbath.\par \par \uc2 The worker, a member of the United Church of God, was a manager at Hatch Trick, a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  franchisee with locations in the Austin, Texas, area. According to the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , the worker's Christian denomination celebrates the sabbath on Saturdays.\par \par \uc2 Hatch Trick initially respected the employee's request, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  said, but later tried to schedule her for Saturday hours. When she appealed to management, the company said it could honor her request if she accepted a lower-paying job with fewer hours and benefits, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  said, and then fired her after she declined.\par \par \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  and Hatch Trick didn't respond to requests to comment.\par \par \uc2 Since taking charge at the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  last year, Chair Andrea Lucas has made it a priority to pursue employers whose actions might interfere with workers expressing their religious beliefs. Lucas, a conservative Christian, has said she believes religious discrimination was overlooked and under-prosecuted in the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 "Religious liberty is a first freedom, not a second-class right," Lucas said in April as the Trump administration released a report on eradicating what it called anti-Christian bias.\par \par \uc2 In April, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  said the agency had filed 16 religious-discrimination lawsuits and recovered over $63 million on behalf of religious workers since January 2025. It said it obtained recoveries for religious workers of over $48 million in fiscal 2025, a 146% increase over the amount in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Among other targets, she has gone after employers that required workers to receive Covid-19 vaccines to return to work. She was also a member of a Trump administration task force set up to study anti-Christian bias in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260518em5i0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
