\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Arkansas Town Drives War Machine --- Region's contractors shepherd workers to factory jobs amid U.S. defense demand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CAMDEN, Ark. -- When the Pentagon put out an urgent call for rocket launchers and ammunition to send to Ukraine and replenish supplies at home in 2022, an answer came from an unlikely place: this remote southern pine-belt town, population around 10,000.\par \par \uc2 The town faced a basic challenge: where to find enough workers to meet a surge in demand for weapons?\par \par \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 , one of the defense contractors clustered in the east part of Camden and already one of the town's biggest employers, was reeling in government orders to produce more mobile rocket launchers. Other big defense contractors in the area soon landed contracts to produce ammunition and expand their facilities in Camden.\par \par \uc2 Some of the local defense companies, including \uc2 L3Harris Technologies\uc2 , found themselves sometimes poaching talent from each other, and realized they were hurting themselves. They were often all part of a single production supply chain.\par \par \uc2 "We were robbing ourselves by robbing our neighbors," said Adam Bailey, a production operations manager for \uc2 Lockheed\uc2  in Camden at the time. "I knew we had to back up and figure out a different way."\par \par \uc2 Lockheed\uc2 's immediate strategy: expand the pool of potential workers. It began inviting local high-school seniors and their parents to its Camden facility for tours. The contractor also started working with Southern Arkansas University Tech, a two-year community college in Camden, to increase apprenticeships, so it could more readily hire people who had never worked at a factory.\par \par \uc2 In return, state officials stepped up workforce-development grants and began funneling millions of dollars to SAU Tech.\par \par \uc2 The efforts largely worked, and have helped turn the area into an engine for the U.S. war machine. As of last year, Ouachita County, where Camden sits, and neighboring Calhoun County employed 3,140 people in their aerospace and defense industries, up about 54% from 2020, according to the Arkansas Department of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 James Lee Silliman, executive director of Ouachita Partnership for Economic Development, said the expanding defense industry is benefiting the community, including small restaurant owners who are getting more visitors.\par \par \uc2 Also, "we're seeing younger engineers right out of college going to work," said Silliman.\par \par \uc2 Camden's identity as a defense manufacturing town dates to 1944, when the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  set up an ammunition depot to produce rockets for World War II. The town sat near major rail lines, which allowed munitions to be transported to each coast.\par \par \uc2 The town suffered in the years following the Cold War, when U.S. military demand waned. The decline in the timber and paper industries, once significant employers in the region, also dented the economy.\par \par \uc2 The city's fortunes began to turn after Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022, as the war helped touch off a new round of investment in U.S. defense.\par \par \uc2 Lockheed\uc2 's Camden facility started recruiting people right out of local high schools. To address their lack of work experience, the firm gave them 2,000 hours of on-the-job training and channeled them to SAU Tech, for 80 hours of coursework.\par \par \uc2 It was an apprenticeship program already in place that the school and company doubled down on as hiring ramped up.\par \par \uc2 Many students saw it as a way to build a career while staying at home, at least to start. During tours of their facility, \uc2 Lockheed\uc2  would tell the high-schoolers that they could make around $36,000 plus benefits -- an attractive proposition given that local schoolteachers were making about that much. Starting salaries at \uc2 Lockheed\uc2  have since increased to around $50,000.\par \par \uc2 "I was surprised stuff like that was in Camden," said Darryl King, 21 years old, who toured the contractor's facility with his grandfather when he was a senior at Camden Fairview High and was part of the company's first class of high-school recruits in 2023. Now, King is one of several assemblers on \uc2 Lockheed\uc2 's PAC-3 missile assembly line.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds more workers will be needed in Camden in the coming years. President Trump is pushing to expand the U.S. defense budget to $1.5 trillion for fiscal 2027, up from the $900 billion authorized for fiscal 2026 by Congress.\par \par \uc2 L3Harris is creating a campus of more than 20 buildings in Camden to increase its large solid-rocket motor manufacturing sixfold; \uc2 Lockheed\uc2  is ramping up interceptor production; and RTX is building a facility as part of a $1.25 billion contract to supply Israel with Tamir missiles.\par \par \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  also plans to open an in-house Munitions Acceleration Center to teach its workers how to program robotic manufacturing systems and adapt to increasingly automated production lines.\par \par \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2 , which ramped up its Camden-based workforce after being awarded contracts in 2023 to produce more 155mm artillery rounds, is training its employees there to scan and test equipment for quality and safety.\par \par \uc2 "Camden would be a ghost town if not for aerospace and defense," said Silliman, who oversees Ouachita County's economic development. "Nobody really likes war or conflict, but it's the reality of the world today."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260518em5i0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
