\par\par \b \uc2 Cross Country: Washington State's Pension Raid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jay Rogers \par \uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Correction\par \par \uc2 The average annuitant in Washington state's Law Enforcement Officers' and Firefighters' Retirement System Plan 1 draws $5,413 a month. The May 16 Cross Country column misstated the payment's frequency.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ May 26, 2026)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 Washington's Gov. Bob Ferguson signed House Bill 2034 on the first day of April. The law formally terminates Law Enforcement Officers' and Firefighters' Retirement System Plan 1, or LEOFF 1, on June 30, 2029. It also creates a successor fund that preserves on paper the benefits of this annuity plan and sweeps the surplus -- nearly $3.9 billion by the state's own projection -- out of the fund. Of that, $569 million flows to a "climate commitment account." The remainder -- close to $3.4 billion -- lands in an account lawmakers can access for general spending starting next year.\par \par \uc2 In simpler terms: The state of Washington, facing an estimated $2 billion budget shortfall, found a pension that was 160% funded and reached for it. This isn't mismanagement or fiscal creativity. It's a decision to raid accounts funding the retirements of public employees to pay for unrelated government spending.\par \par \uc2 LEOFF 1 closed to new members in 1977. Contributions from state, local employers and employees were suspended around 2000 after the plan became fully funded. The surplus didn't grow through overcontribution or accounting error. It grew because the \uc2 Washington State Investment Board\uc2  chose capable managers, balanced risk and compounded returns inside a commingled trust over decades. The 2024 actuarial valuation put the plan at 160% funded, with 5,945 annuitants and only six active members remaining.\par \par \uc2 The state House passed HB 2034 on Feb. 13, 2026, by 55-39, with every yes vote coming from Democrats. Three Democrats crossed the aisle and joined Republicans in opposition. In the Senate, the bill passed 25-22 with no Republican voting in favor.\par \par \uc2 The statutory case against the law is direct. State law says plainly that all funds in the LEOFF account "shall remain in that fund for the purpose of paying the obligations of the fund."\par \par \uc2 Defenders argue that roughly 80% of historical contributions came from state appropriations, so the excess belongs to taxpayers. But a contribution to a pension fund creates a legal obligation to specific beneficiaries, not a discretionary reserve the Legislature can draw on whenever the budget runs short.\par \par \uc2 State actuaries have flagged the resulting risk. A 2,000-scenario stress test found a significant increase in the share of cases in which LEOFF 1 develops an unfunded liability after the sweep. The law's 110% funding buffer sounds conservative until you run it through a 2008-style market event or another prolonged period of underperformance. The plan, which operated without requesting a dollar of new contributions for more than two decades, now carries measurably higher tail risk -- which would be absorbed by future taxpayers, not the legislators who structured the transaction.\par \par \uc2 A constitutional challenge is already in federal court. Nine named plaintiffs filed a class-action complaint on April 30 in the U.S. District Court for the Western District of Washington. Lead plaintiff \uc2 Dave Reichert\uc2  -- a former King County Sheriff and a LEOFF 1 member since 1972 -- is joined by John Nordlund, president of the Retired Seattle Police Officers' Association, and Michael Duchemin, president of the Retired Firefighters of Washington. The complaint argues that HB 2034 violates the Contract Clauses of both the U.S. and Washington constitutions. The plaintiffs have asked the court to block all transfers.\par \par \uc2 When a deep-blue state legislature establishes the precedent that surplus assets in a solvent public pension can be tapped for general use, every budget-constrained statehouse will take notice. Actuarial success stops being an achievement and becomes instead a liability. A pension fund that did everything right has been terminated to fill a budget gap.\par \par \uc2 Washington state perfectly illustrates the danger of defined-benefit plans. Politicians can't reach into personal accounts the way they can reach into a pooled trust like LEOFF 1. The average LEOFF 1 annuitant draws only $5,413 a year, less than $500 a month. The fund holding that obligation grew to nearly $4 billion above actuarial requirements. Lawmakers looked at that figure and lunged.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rogers is president of \uc2 Alpha Strategies Investment Consulting\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260516em5g0006g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
