\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Scandal Was Already Messy. AI's Making It Worse. --- First came the sexual-assault lawsuit by ex-employee. Then came the deepfakes.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tatum Hunter \par \uc2 863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 A 2020 study by researchers in the \uc2 University of Texas System\uc2  found that including an image with a tweet more than doubled its reshares. A Review article on Saturday about deepfake videos incorrectly said researchers at \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  conducted the study.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ May 22, 2026)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 The internet has always had a problem with the truth. False rumors and smear campaigns have spread across social media for two decades, destroying reputations in their wake. But events over the past week suggest that what's coming is even worse: AI-generated video that supercharges online harassment.\par \par \uc2 Just days after an ex-\uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  employee filed a sexual assault and discrimination lawsuit alleging a female co-worker at the bank demanded to have sex with him and threatened his career advancement if he didn't, "deepfake" videos of the two parties spread across X and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , racking up millions of views.\par \par \uc2 One clip shows the female executive dancing suggestively in an office while male co-workers shower her with dollar bills. In another, she caresses the ex-employee while he works at a desk. ("50 Shades of \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 ," the post says.) In a third, they are at the Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 A particularly alarming example depicts the two parties through the window of a cafe, smiling together over a glass of wine. The clip is insidious in part because it is not outrageous: It looks as if it captures a private moment. At the end of the video, the couple notice they are being watched and look worried, as if they have been caught.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said it conducted an internal investigation and found no support for the ex-employee's claims. The female employee's legal team said the accusations are false and that she and the ex-employee never had a romantic or sexual relationship. Both sides are gearing up for a possible trial. And yet, the images sweeping the internet are creating lurid fictional narratives about the two people at the center of the case, in some cases grabbing details straight from the plaintiff's complaint to paint a dubious portrait of everyone involved.\par \par \uc2 "We believe images," said \uc2 Danielle Citron\uc2 , a law professor at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  who studies online abuse and privacy. "They viscerally hit us."\par \par \uc2 This can be true even when the AI-generated imagery is glitchy, cartoonish or jokey. A 2025 study by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  found that humans correctly guess whether an image is real or generated only 62% of the time -- slightly better than a coin flip. Because people struggle to spot AI images, it could influence our perception of real-world events, another 2023 study suggests.\par \par \uc2 The digital world is "taking the likenesses of these two people and using them in a way that has a life of its own," said Jeremy Carrasco, director at independent media watchdog Riddance, which helps people spot AI content.\par \par \uc2 Making AI images is easy. It only takes a minute or two to generate a fake video of a real person using a chatbot like X's Grok. As a result, this kind of media will likely play a growing role in the manufacturing of online scandals, said Renee DiResta, a researcher who studies internet shame campaigns.\par \par \uc2 One 2020 study by researchers at \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  found that including an image with a tweet more than doubled its reshares. When those visuals stoke outrage or invoke a deep-seated bias, onlookers are even more likely to engage. Content creators, who make money from views, are likely to add their own commentary or conjecture to any controversy that promises to bring in more eyeballs.\par \par \uc2 Citron said the unfolding incident at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  immediately made her think back to a defamation case the actor Johnny Depp brought against his ex-wife Amber Heard after she wrote a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  op-ed that described herself as a "public figure representing domestic abuse."\par \par \uc2 That trial became the center of a monthslong online media circus. Major \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 TikTok\uc2  creators -- most without any legal background whatsoever -- pumped out hundreds of videos dramatizing each step of the trial. The videos drew audiences in the millions, which impacted the public's perception of the case.\par \par \uc2 And that was before the era of AI videos, Citron noted.\par \par \uc2 There is very little established recourse for civilians who find themselves deepfaked. But a class-action lawsuit filed in January is taking aim at \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 xAI\uc2  after the company's chatbot Grok allowed people to flood social media with millions of digitally altered nude images of real women and children. The lawsuit cites multiple grievances, including privacy violations, negligence and defamation. Ashley St. Clair, a content creator and the mother of one of Musk's children, also sued \uc2 xAI\uc2  this year over sexualized deepfakes depicting her.\par \par \uc2 The consequences of these lawsuits could be wide-ranging, according to Citron, potentially changing how tech companies view their own legal liability or, conversely, how safe victims feel speaking out after they're targeted with an AI smear campaign.\par \par \uc2 But the outcome won't change the incentives that make AI video useful for professional and amateur internet-posters -- in addition to those who seek to shape public opinion in much more powerful ways. "We're going to see more weaponization of this kind of synthetic lie," Citron said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260516em5g0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
