\par\par \b \uc2 WPA Meets AI in My Work for America Proposal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ro Khanna \par \uc2 792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I asked students at Brown University a few weeks ago: "How many of you are worried about getting a job?" Every hand went up.\par \par \uc2 Brown is an elite institution with historically strong career pathways. Yet even there students are losing confidence. When I asked why, the answer was unanimous: artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Companies like \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Oracle\uc2  are cutting roles even as profits grow. A recent Stanford study, "Canaries in the Coal Mine," found that young workers in the most AI-exposed occupations saw a 16% relative decline in employment from late 2022 through September 2025.\par \par \uc2 Joblessness in your 20s reduces lifetime earnings, narrows your prospects, carries mental-health risks and weakens your sense of purpose. This isn't only a labor-market disruption. It's a generational emergency.\par \par \uc2 We have missed such warnings before. In the 1980s, William Julius Wilson sounded the alarm on job loss hollowing out black communities. In 2015, economists \uc2 Anne Case\uc2  and \uc2 Angus Deaton\uc2  documented deaths of despair in working-class America.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's \uc2 Dario Amodei\uc2  has warned of massive job loss across technology, finance, law and consulting, with unemployment surging as high as 20%. The risks go beyond white-collar work; manufacturing and other sectors also face pressures.\par \par \uc2 We need the boldest jobs agenda in generations. I call it Work for America. Work for America is inspired by President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2 's Works Progress Administration. Employing more than 8.5 million people, WPA built bridges, roads, public buildings, parks and airports, while supporting artists, writers and musicians whose work helped define American culture.\par \par \uc2 Critics called it a boondoggle, but WPA got millions of Americans back to work. It boosted America's recovery from the Great Depression while creating physical and cultural infrastructure we still rely on today.\par \par \uc2 During the Depression, our nation experienced a total collapse in demand, not a massive technological change. But again the private market is failing to absorb willing workers. And the cost will be disproportionately borne by entry-level workers doing repetitive tasks.\par \par \uc2 Work for America would provide one million living-wage jobs for up to three years across a range of occupations at an estimated cost of $62 billion annually, which is less than 1% of the federal budget. Open to all ages, it would attract young people and function like a national-service program. It would begin not in stages but at scale to match the emergency, with the capacity to grow if displacement gets worse. Participants would develop skills to work in the private and nonprofit sectors.\par \par \uc2 Work for America would rebuild the ranks of public service -- not only in Washington, but among the teachers, social workers, public-health workers, and local government staff who make our communities function. A new generation of civil servants can overhaul websites, cut through red tape and improve agency collaboration.\par \par \uc2 Young people can serve in their own communities or far from home, building connections across social divides. As a young lawyer, my formative experiences were in Biloxi, Miss., representing families who lost their homes to Hurricane Katrina. As a young federal employee, my economic ideas were shaped by visiting shuttered mills in Youngstown, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Work for America will recruit Americans for moon-shot projects on renewable energy, cancer research, AI ethics and quantum computing. During World War II, FDR recruited William Knudsen, president of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , to help mobilize American industry. Work for America will build similar partnerships, funding the hiring and training of people in key sectors, doing work the private market isn't doing, in places it hasn't reached.\par \par \uc2 I have established pilot programs with companies like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Zoom\uc2  that demonstrate how these partnerships can work. Companies can join with community colleges, historically black colleges and local institutions to train workers for high-paying AI-era jobs using AI tools. Work for America would fund these programs nationally.\par \par \uc2 Labor and vocational training will be crucial. Creating programs with unions, Work for America would establish 1,000 new trade schools across the country. To ensure that those pathways lead to jobs, it would encourage federal contractors to develop apprenticeship programs.\par \par \uc2 The companies driving the AI revolution are generating enormous wealth. A modest tax on billionaires and a token tax on AI use will more than suffice to finance Work for America.\par \par \uc2 Walt Whitman wrote that he heard America singing in the carols of mechanics, carpenters, and shoemakers building our country. That chorus has grown quiet in recent decades, muted by offshoring, automation and economic dislocation.\par \par \uc2 Work for America would help us hear it again, loud and hopeful, from a generation determined to build our country anew.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Khanna, a Democrat, represents California's 17th Congressional District.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260515em5f00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
