\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Cisco\uc2  to Cut About 5% of Staff to Free Up Cash for AI\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cisco Systems\uc2  is cutting thousands of jobs to make room for more artificial intelligence in its business.\par \par \uc2 The technology conglomerate said Wednesday that it plans to eliminate fewer than 4,000 jobs this quarter, less than 5% of its workforce, so that it can pour more resources into silicon, optics, security and AI.\par \par \uc2 "We don't always have the exact resources that we need going forward in the right places," Chief Executive \uc2 Chuck Robbins\uc2  said on a call with analysts. "That's really what this is about versus savings."\par \par \uc2 The restructuring is expected to cost as much as $1 billion in severance, one-time termination benefits and other expenses. Layoff notifications will start going out on Thursday, Robbins said in a blog post.\par \par \uc2 The network-equipment company is the latest tech player to trim its head count this year to free up more cash for investments in AI. \uc2 Oracle\uc2  slashed tens of thousands of jobs in March amid a build-out of costly data centers. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  said in January that it would lay off 16,000 corporate employees.\par \par \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's overhaul was announced in tandem with its latest quarterly earnings report, in which it raised full-year guidance on the back of big revenue and earnings gains. Shares climbed 17% to $119.11 in aftermarket trading.\par \par \uc2 The San Jose, Calif., company took $1.9 billion in AI infrastructure orders from hyperscalers in its fiscal third quarter, which ended on April 25, compared with $600 million a year earlier. Year-to-date orders from hyperscalers have already exceeded the $5 billion target \uc2 Cisco\uc2  had set for all of fiscal 2026, with a full quarter left to go, Robbins said.\par \par \uc2 The company expects $62.8 billion to $63 billion in revenue this fiscal year, which ends in late July. That is up from a forecast in February for $61.2 billion to $61.7 billion. Earnings should hit $3.16 to $3.21 a share for the full fiscal year, instead of $3 to $3.08 as previously projected, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  said. Adjusted earnings are on track to reach $4.27 to $4.29 a share, up from its prior forecast of $4.13 to $4.17 a share. In the latest fiscal quarter, revenue rose 12% to $15.84 billion.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260514em5e0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
