\uc2 Newsmax\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Energy Storage\par \par \uc2 Powers Ford\par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 's stock soared Wednesday as Wall Street cheered the automaker's increased focus on using batteries once meant for electric vehicles as stationary energy storage systems.\par \par \uc2 Shares gained 13% to close at $13.57, giving the automaker's stock its biggest one-day percentage gain since March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analysts cited the company's new Ford Energy subsidiary in a note Wednesday as an "underappreciated" competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 Energy storage systems are large stationary batteries, which have emerged as an alternative business strategy for carmakers that invested heavily in electric-vehicle battery plants. Demand for EVs has slowed in recent years amid U.S. policy changes.\par \par \uc2 The automaker said this week that Ford Energy will provide battery storage systems for utilities, data centers and large industrial and commercial customers across the U.S. Ford has said it would invest $2 billion into the business.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  believes there is a "fairly high likelihood" that Ford signs a supply agreement with large commercial customers over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 In particular, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cited the automaker's agreement to license technology from Chinese company CATL, the world's largest EV battery maker, as a key advantage, and estimated Ford Energy could be worth $10 billion.\par \par \uc2 -- Ryan Felton\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260514em5e00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
