\par\par \b \uc2 Jobs at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  Turned Into Huge Jackpots for Employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin \par \uc2 665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  allowed employees to sell up to $30 million worth of shares each in a recent financing, making them some of the earliest financial winners of the artificial-intelligence boom.\par \par \uc2 Last October, more than 600 current and former employees sold their shares in a single stroke, collectively making $6.6 billion. For about 75 of them, that meant walking away with the full $30 million, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Some of them chose to give away the rest, putting their remaining shares in donor-advised funds -- charitable investment accounts that commit money for philanthropic causes while also allowing donors to claim tax deductions for that year.\par \par \uc2 The sale offers a sneak peek into the flood of money that will soon hit San Francisco and other tech capitals. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  are gearing up for what will likely be some of the largest IPOs in history, allowing their thousands of rank-and-file workers to unload their stock, turning many of them into multimillionaires.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  required employees to wait two years before they could sell their shares, meaning that the share sale marked the first time many who joined the company after ChatGPT launched were able to cash out. No other tech boom in history has lavished that magnitude of wealth on such a swath of employees even before a public listing. Hundreds of companies went public in the dot-com boom, but in most cases their workers had to wait for a prolonged period even after the IPO to cash in. For some, the bubble burst before they could do so and they never realized the potential wealth.\par \par \uc2 The scale of AI pay packages for some highly specialized workers has been unprecedented. While early employees at \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  made millions of dollars after the companies went public, the scope of wealth creation for some AI specialists has reached greater heights.\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 Meta\uc2  offered $300 million pay packages to some top researchers as part of a broader industrywide talent war. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  offers yearly salaries that top $500,000 for some technical roles, according to its website, and is doling out far more stock-based compensation than other tech companies. Last August, it gave some staff members one-time bonuses, some of which were worth millions of dollars, The Wall Street Journal reported.\par \par \uc2 The newfound wealth is driving up rental prices in San Francisco, and sparking concerns about a growing class divide within the city. Some top AI executives have pledged to provide a large portion of their earnings to charity, alongside rank-and-file employees.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employees who were at the company when it first issued shares seven years ago have seen the value of their stock grow more than 100-fold. By comparison, the Nasdaq composite roughly tripled in the same period.\par \par \uc2 For most of Silicon Valley's history, startup employees had to wait for an initial public offering before they could sell their shares. But as companies began staying private longer, some workers found themselves sitting on paper fortunes they couldn't touch for a long time. That led to the rising popularity of so-called tender offers, whereby employees could sell a slice of their shares to outside investors.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has overseen several tender offers in recent years, but previously limited sales to $10 million per employee, frustrating some researchers and engineers who were eligible to sell far more. The company said it tripled the cap last fall in response to demand from investors.\par \par \uc2 Top \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  executives have experienced even more of a windfall. President \uc2 Greg Brockman\uc2  holds equity worth about $30 billion, he said Monday during court testimony. Chief Executive \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  has said he doesn't own shares in the company, citing its nonprofit roots, though some investors expect him to receive equity if he prevails in a court battle with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  over \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's restructuring from a nonprofit into a for-profit company.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich386\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal5790
