\par\par \b \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Learns Hard Way Art of Payoff to Avert Scandal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An employee comes forward with embarrassing, legally fraught workplace allegations. For company bosses, it presents a thorny dilemma: fight or pay?\par \par \uc2 It is a situation \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  found itself in weeks before a former banker filed a lawsuit filled with sensational accusations. It offered $1 million to settle his claims that a female colleague had sexually harassed him and more, The Wall Street Journal reported, even though the bank has investigated and said it doesn't believe the claims have merit.\par \par \uc2 Though such deals rarely come to light, paying employees to make potential scandals go away isn't an uncommon practice at American corporations. Companies have long offered settlements to head off litigation or avoid claims that could tarnish its reputation -- even if executives conclude the allegations lack merit.\par \par \uc2 "The vast majority of settlements are business decisions," said Bill Stein, a Los Angeles-based partner at employment law firm Fisher Phillips, who often works with companies on such matters. Some employers, wanting to avoid the time, expense and headaches of unwanted publicity and litigation, ask: "Can we just make this go away?"\par \par \uc2 The calculations over when and how much to negotiate have grown more complicated now that AI and social media can easily amplify such allegations. Often the decision-making involves external law firms, internal investigations and discussions with everyone from human-resources specialists to strategists in crisis public relations, executives and attorneys say. Internal complaints -- about anything from bullying and policy violations to performance issues and workplace violence -- are surfacing with increasing frequency, data suggest. Allegations of discrimination, harassment and retaliation rose to 14.7 per 1,000 employees in 2024, from 6.4 in 2021, according to \uc2 HR Acuity\uc2 , a company that helps employers track internal complaints and investigations.\par \par \uc2 "It happens every day of the week" at large companies, said \uc2 Janine Yancey\uc2 , an employment lawyer and founder of HR compliance firm Emtrain.\par \par \uc2 The decision on whether to offer a payout usually begins with a question: Is there truth to the claims?\par \par \uc2 Employers will often attempt to quickly investigate the matter, usually by interviewing those involved or pulling emails, texts and chat messages between colleagues on work devices. If companies find the allegations are at least partly substantiated, they are likely to offer a more generous settlement, lawyers and HR executives say.\par \par \uc2 In other cases, companies may feel the allegations are bunk -- but still opt to settle. That can be for any number of reasons. Say a startup is trying to raise new funding, Fisher Phillips's Stein said. The last thing they may want is to spook investors with a potential lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's offer to settle with Chirayu Rana didn't keep his claims from going viral. He alleged a female colleague sexually harassed and assaulted him and that co-workers had subjected him to racial discrimination. Rana rejected the $1 million settlement offer -- equivalent to less than two years of his compensation at the bank -- and asked for more money, the Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 After Rana filed the suit in a New York state court, AI-generated videos of Rana and the female banker quickly proliferated across the internet.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers for the female colleague, named in the lawsuit as Lorna Hajdini, have said they are entirely made up and that the two never had any sexual relations. An attorney for Rana didn't respond to a request for comment but has said the truth would come to light in court proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260512em5c0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
