\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Gas Prices Take Toll on 'Supercommuters' --- Long drives and less work-from-home time are producing pain at the pump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nicole Smith fills the gas tank of her 2021 Jeep Compass three times a week to keep up with her long commute, 50 miles each way, three times a week, from her Fredericksburg, Va., home to a graduate program in the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 Smith, 35 years old, and her husband moved there from a town closer to Washington, D.C., in 2023 for more-affordable housing. Now she is being stung by higher gasoline prices, with fill-ups costing about $200 more each month compared with earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 "One of the biggest things that I have been forgoing is just less fun activities, less weekends out, less traveling," said Smith, who is retired from the D.C. Army National Guard and studying public health. She and her husband live off his Army paycheck and her disability benefits.\par \par \uc2 "It's mostly frustrating because you go from one month budgeting for a certain amount, and the next month it's another amount," said Smith, who can spend up to two hours fighting traffic in each one-way commute.\par \par \uc2 The ranks of America's marathon commuters have grown since the pandemic, with many workers moving farther away from city centers for cheaper housing and more space. The work-from-home era, meanwhile, has shifted to more of a full-time and hybrid-schedule mix. These days so-called supercommuters, often defined as people who travel 90 minutes or more each way to work, are paying an especially heavy price if they have to drive.\par \par \uc2 Commuters who drive a new vehicle with average fuel economy 75 miles each way, every weekday, could spend roughly $500 a month to keep their tanks filled just for commutes at today's prices, if prices stay elevated. Add in the extra amount in high-cost states such as California and more miles, and expenses balloon from there.\par \par \uc2 The national average for regular gasoline reached $4.54 a gallon on Wednesday, according to AAA, up 52% since the initial U.S.-Israeli attack of Iran at the end of February. Around the U.S., averages range from $3.96 in Oklahoma to $6.16 in California.\par \par \uc2 A 2024 study from researchers at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  and INRIX found that across the 10 largest cities in the U.S., the number of people with driving commutes of 75 miles or more -- the study's definition of a supercommute -- increased by about a third after the pandemic hit four years earlier. Working from home exploded in 2020, dropped from 2021 to 2023 and then from 2024 onward has been flat, indicating that the study's finding still holds, said \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , one of the authors.\par \par \uc2 There is evidence that at least some Americans are now trying to spend more time in home offices, he added. Work-from-home days rose from 24.7% of days this past February to 26.9% of days in March, according to Bloom's data.\par \par \uc2 "Another way of putting this is if about 150 million Americans are working on any given weekday, an increase of 2.2% days in WFH means an extra three million Americans working from home," Bloom said.\par \par \uc2 Still, nearly 63% of workers are now fully on-site, up from just over half in late 2021, according to the Stanford data. Many other workers have to commute for at least some of the workweeks.\par \par \uc2 Ivan Lamptey, an accountant in Sacramento, Calif., leaves home at 5 a.m. five days a week to drive about 120 miles each way to work in Fremont, a city in the San Francisco Bay Area. His 2020 \uc2 BMW\uc2  3 Series takes premium gasoline, which now costs more than $6.50 a gallon on average around the state. All in all, Lamptey estimates he is now paying about $1,600 a month for fuel and tolls.\par \par \uc2 He said he is "being stubborn" by not cutting back on other expenses thus far, despite the gasoline surge. "As this progresses, I might have to change my spring and summer plans" for leisure travel, said Lamptey, 50. "I'm more annoyed than anything," he added.\par \par \uc2 Danielle Grossman, 37, commutes 90 minutes each way, three days a week, from her home north of Houston to the Houston Livestock Show and Rodeo, where she works as communications director. She recently traded in a Honda Passport SUV for a new Kia Telluride hybrid, hoping to save on fuel costs, and is also watching other spending.\par \par \uc2 "If we have a date night or if we have to do anything, we avoid driving into town at all costs because it adds up," she said.\par \par \uc2 Higher gasoline prices act as a tax on consumers, according to economists. New Federal Reserve Bank of New York research, released Wednesday, found the surge in fuel costs has hit lower-income households particularly hard, prompting them to rein in spending. This follows data from the \uc2 Bank of America Institute\uc2  showing that lower-income consumers curbed spending on such categories as restaurants, leisure and travel in March as higher fuel prices ate into their budgets.\par \par \uc2 In past periods in which the share of household spending on gasoline increased, including 2009 to 2011 and 2016 to 2018, the bank's research arm found that consumers traded down to cheaper groceries and cut back on buying such things as electronics and furniture.\par \par \uc2 Carolyn Staats has a huge commute: 100 miles each way, four times a week, from her home in Los Banos, Calif., to her job as a physical therapist's aide in the Bay Area. She drives a 2025 Honda Accord hybrid, but the heavy mileage is still adding up.\par \par \uc2 "It hurts," said Staats, who now starts her workday by checking the app GasBuddy and is going without a summer-vacation trip this year.\par \par \uc2 "I've had to budget my money more, where instead of saving $150 for gas on a two-week basis it's now $260 -- that extra $110, I could have used it toward my groceries or toward other bills," she said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
