\par\par \b \uc2 JPM\uc2  Offered $1 Million To Stave Off Sex Suit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexander Saeedy \par \uc2 986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  offered $1 million to settle sexual assault and harassment claims brought by a former investment banker weeks before he filed a lawsuit filled with lurid allegations that have captivated Wall Street, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Chirayu Rana's lawsuit in New York state court, which was refiled on Monday after being taken down for a week, alleged that a female colleague sexually assaulted him and that co-workers inside the nation's biggest bank subjected him to racial discrimination.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  has said it conducted an internal investigation including multiple employees and doesn't believe there is merit to his claims. The lawyers for the female colleague, named in the lawsuit as Lorna Hajdini, have said they are entirely made up and that the two never had any sexual relations.\par \par \uc2 The allegations went viral after news reports of the suit's claims. AI-generated videos of Rana and the female banker proliferated on X and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , bringing to life scenarios and dialogue from the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The dispute over the validity of the claims and Rana's credibility only fueled public speculation over the past week, with podcasters like Joe Rogan and Megyn Kelly opining about what really happened.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's offer to settle for $1 million, equivalent to less than two years of his compensation at the bank, was aimed at avoiding all of that, but Rana rejected it and asked for more money, the people said.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, a \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  spokesman said, "We did try to reach an agreement to avoid the time and expense of litigation and to support an employee who was being threatened with the very reputational harm now unfolding. We continue to believe these allegations have no merit and new information raised as a result of the public filing only reinforces that conclusion."\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's move to try to settle Rana's claims offers a window into the rarely seen world of negotiations over sensitive allegations brought by employees and shows the complex calculus companies face when dealing with them.\par \par \uc2 Even if companies conclude there is no substance to harassment claims, they will in many cases decide to cut checks to avoid protracted disputes playing out in public view.\par \par \uc2 Rana started working at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's leveraged finance team originating new loans in May 2024 as a senior vice president, the suit said.\par \par \uc2 In May 2025, Rana filed an internal complaint to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's human resources department alleging that he had been the victim of discrimination, race-based harassment and sexual assault at the hands of fellow employees at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , the suit said.\par \par \uc2 He said in the suit that colleagues directed racially based epithets related to his Nepalese background and that he was sexually assaulted multiple times by a senior-ranking female employee.\par \par \uc2 Rana was put on paid leave after the complaint was made, and the bank conducted an internal investigation, according to the suit.\par \par \uc2 The bank spent months on the review but lacked specifics to corroborate allegations in the complaint, one person familiar with the matter said. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  has said Rana didn't cooperate with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Rana left \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and started a new job at private-equity firm \uc2 Bregal Sagemount\uc2  in October 2025, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Rana and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  started weeks of mediation talks in early 2026, the lawsuit said. In March, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  offered Rana $1 million to settle his claims, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Rana didn't accept or reject the settlement offer. He filed a discrimination complaint against \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  with the \uc2 U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  at the end of March, the people said.\par \par \uc2 In early April, he was let go from his new job at \uc2 Bregal Sagemount\uc2 , the people said. The firm said he was no longer an employee and that "during his employment, \uc2 Bregal Sagemount\uc2  was not aware of any complaints he filed against any former employers, other entities, or individuals."\par \par \uc2 In April, Rana's lawyers countered the $1 million offer with a proposal to settle the dispute for $11.75 million, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Rana's lawyers filed a lawsuit in New York state court using the pseudonym John Doe. It was later taken off the court docket, fueling questions about its credibility. The lawyers filed a corrected version that was substantially the same on Monday. The suit includes two affidavits from witnesses whose names are redacted who claim they saw Rana and the accused banker together in a romantic context.\par \par \uc2 "The original lawsuit was not withdrawn," said Daniel Kaiser, Rana's lawyer. "After filing, the court clerk informed us that the suit required review and sign-off from the judge before being formally filed under a pseudonym. Upon signature by the judge yesterday, the suit was formally filed under a pseudonym."\par \par \uc2 The public suit includes graphic and extensive details of his allegations that a more senior banker on the team repeatedly demanded to have sex with him, often with racial insults mixed in, and threatened his career advancement if he didn't engage.\par \par \uc2 He alleged she forcibly performed oral sex on him and that they had sex only under duress while he begged her not to.\par \par \uc2 "If you don't f--- me soon, I'm going to ruin you. . .Never forget, I f---ing own you," she said, according to the suit. "If you don't f--- my brains out tonight, I'm going to sabotage your promotion."\par \par \uc2 At one point, the suit alleged: "She then told Plaintiff to suck her toes, repeating that she would facilitate his promotion and bonus."\par \par \uc2 Through a JPMorgan spokesman, Hajdini's lawyers said she "continues to categorically deny the allegations. She never dated this individual, never had a sexual or romantic encounter with him of any kind and never gave him any drugs. She maintains that his false claims are entirely fabricated and tarnishing her reputation."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260507em5700017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
