\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 IBM\uc2  CEO Says AI Requires Big Shift\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Rosenbush \par \uc2 694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2  CEO \uc2 Arvind Krishna\uc2  says maximizing returns on investment in artificial intelligence requires a fundamental restructuring of business workflows rather than just adopting new technology.\par \par \uc2 The use of AI within a company typically evolves from the individual contributor to small teams, cross-functional groups and ultimately the entire organization. As a company progresses from one stage to another, so do the potential returns on AI investment. That is less about technology alone than it is about updating age-old processes and social dynamics, according to Krishna.\par \par \uc2 "In the next year or two, the enterprise world will sort into two camps: companies where AI runs their business, and companies where AI is still a project," Krishna said.\par \par \uc2 The line between those companies that make broader use of AI and those that don't won't simply come down to technology. "It will be their operating model," Krishna said.\par \par \uc2 IBM\uc2  on Tuesday at its Think conference in Boston announced a slew of products and capabilities, including a new version of watsonx Orchestrate, a secure multiagent control plane, and IBM Bob, for securely building and deploying agents.\par \par \uc2 While model developers are competing to stay one step ahead of each other, \uc2 IBM\uc2  is approaching AI from a different angle, helping its clients scale their AI efforts. AI is a core part of the technology giant's strategy.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 IBM\uc2  reported higher first-quarter revenue of $15.92 billion and higher profit, driven by growing adoption of artificial-intelligence tools. While the numbers were ahead of expectations, the stock price fell. To some extent, that reflects the fact that \uc2 IBM\uc2  has been caught up in broader AI-driven concerns about software, but \uc2 Ben Reitzes\uc2  of equity research and consulting firm Melius Research has a buy rating on the company. "I'm excited . . . to see their AI-related business come of age," he said.\par \par \uc2 The use of AI at many companies began in earnest in recent years with proofs of concept. The experimentation stage has led to the greenlighting of many projects. Now companies are looking to move to the next stage, or enterprisewide deployment of AI. That s potentially more rewarding, but also much more complex from an organizational perspective.\par \par \uc2 Krishna cited the evolution of \uc2 IBM\uc2 's internal human-resource processes as an example of the operating model behind enterprise-level scaling.\par \par \uc2 In the pre-AI era, Krishna said, if an employee requested an employment verification letter to support an apartment rental application, the workflow required up to 18 different human touchpoints, including a manager, an HR business specialist, back-office staff and multiple software systems.\par \par \uc2 Today, an employee can generate the letter by making a request to an internal bot called "Ask HR." The AI agent, integrated into \uc2 IBM\uc2 's security network, automatically verifies the employee's identity, pulls the required data from the HR system and sends the letter. All the employee has to do is specify how the letter should be delivered. The 18 touchpoints have been reduced to just one, according to Krishna.\par \par \uc2 To enable AI to scale across the enterprise, processes need to be redesigned end to end, according to Krishna. \uc2 IBM\uc2 's Project Bob, as the initiative was known internally before it became a product, was designed to manage the entire software development life cycle, from writing new code to patching old code, generating documentation, creating test cases and ensuring security compliance, he said.\par \par \uc2 "I don't begin with eliminating steps. I begin with how many touch points can I take out? And how can I make it much more nimble and faster and end-to-end? That's the goal. Out of that comes the fact that you should eliminate steps," Krishna said.\par \par \uc2 If people lose their jobs as a result, "I get to redeploy them, to do something else of more value," Krishna said.\par \par \uc2 IBM\uc2  has made progress rethinking its operating models, but more work lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's early days. We're only a third of the way through what can be done," he said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Steven Rosenbush writes for WSJ Leadership Institute's CIO Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260506em560008i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
