\par\par \b \uc2 AI's Stark Choice: Cut Jobs Or Stretch Workforce --- Split emerges among bosses over best use of staff as technology increases efficiency\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter and Lindsay Ellis \par \uc2 1,023 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Armstrong\uc2  says his company is cutting 14% of its workforce as AI changes "how we work." \uc2 PayPal\uc2  plans to cut 20% of its staff over the next two to three years, part of plans to step up its AI adoption.\par \par \uc2 Then there is \uc2 Axon\uc2  Enterprise President \uc2 Josh Isner\uc2 , who recently emailed the Taser maker's more than 5,000 staffers and told them to essentially breathe: Artificial intelligence won't trigger layoffs anytime soon, he assured them.\par \par \uc2 "I am thinking of AI as the thing that allows our teams to do more, not the thing that replaces our teams," Isner wrote. Even if the technology lets staff be twice, or even three times, as productive, there will be additional problems to solve. So, he added: "Block out the noise and keep kicking ass."\par \par \uc2 The message arrived at a moment of extreme anxiety in Corporate America over how many jobs could be lost to AI's powers to speed up and supplant much of white-collar work. Among executives making such decisions, a split is emerging: Use AI to shrink the workforce, or stretch it?\par \par \uc2 Those diverging philosophies have been on display on earnings calls and in other recent announcements. Armstrong said Tuesday that \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  would eliminate hundreds of jobs as AI becomes more embedded in the cryptocurrency exchange's operations. Staff will manage agents to do more of the work, he said.\par \par \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2 's CEO told investors last week that with AI, "we're going to experience significant reduction in head count." \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief financial officer, \uc2 Susan Li\uc2 , questioned how many employees the social-media company would eventually need as AI is able to do more.\par \par \uc2 "We don't really know what the optimal size of the company will be in the future," she told investors last Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 At the other end of the spectrum are companies that say they can keep head counts flat -- not necessarily hiring, but not firing, either -- by achieving more with existing teams.\par \par \uc2 Spotify\uc2  co-CEO \uc2 Gustav Soderstrom\uc2  puts the choice companies face like this: They can translate productivity improvements right away into cost savings by trimming staff. Or, "The other thing you could do is to say we're going to be roughly the same amount of people -- we're just going to do more."\par \par \uc2 Spotify\uc2  is doing the latter: "We're keeping our head count roughly flat and just doing much more shipping, more value to consumers," he said.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders risk missing out if their use of AI is overly focused on efficiencies, said \uc2 Nickle LaMoreaux\uc2 , chief human resources officer at \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In your leadership discussions, are you having this idea of moving from AI to productivity to AI to growth?" she said in a recent interview. Though it's hard to predict how many people \uc2 IBM\uc2  will employ three years from now, "if I had a crystal ball," LaMoreaux said, it would be "more."\par \par \uc2 Yet some companies have more room to maneuver than others. At \uc2 Meta\uc2 , massive investments in data centers and other AI infrastructure are driving plans to lay off 8,000 people -- about 10% of the workforce -- CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  has said. Mass layoffs are also a swift way to boost the bottom line, and a flagging stock price. Block and Snap shares both jumped following AI-related job cuts.\par \par \uc2 About 80% of companies using AI agents, intelligent automation or autonomous technologies said they are cutting staff, according to a recent \uc2 Gartner\uc2  survey of 350 people in midlevel positions and above.\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 's Armstrong told employees that though there would be fewer staff, AI would allow them to get more work done. Getting leaner was necessary in a down crypto market, he said. Plus, the restructuring would better position the company for future growth.\par \par \uc2 "Over the past year, I've watched engineers use AI to ship in days what used to take a team weeks," he wrote to staff. "This is a new way of working."\par \par \uc2 Justin Briley, who was laid off Tuesday from his content-strategy and technical writing role at \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , questioned whether fewer staff would enable growth. "We were already pretty bare bones," the 39-year-old said of his team.\par \par \uc2 Briley said he used AI to find efficiencies while working on the back-end of the company's help website, and the technology allowed him to do more.\par \par \uc2 "AI has been marketed as a solution to labor," he said, though he predicts the technology will ultimately create "more work, not less."\par \par \uc2 At companies that have said they are committed to keeping staffers, change is still coming. Many positions will look much different or combine the responsibilities of a few roles at once, human-resources specialists said.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Synchrony Financial\uc2 , which issues credit cards on behalf of companies like \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , Sam's Club and Lowe's, HR chief \uc2 DJ Casto\uc2  said he is already coaching employees to prepare not for layoffs, but for "redeployments" to new assignments. In some cases, they might be permanent shifts; others could be just for a few months.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to have to be a lot more agile," Casto said. "I talk about this openly in our workforce: We're going to have to be a lot more comfortable that it's not going to be so black and white."\par \par \uc2 At Axon, some managers said their teams have anxiety about potential job cuts, Isner said. Axon makes software in addition to security products such as Taser and body cameras, and its shares have fallen about 30% this year on investor jitters that AI could fuel a software apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 In his note to staff, Isner said the company's business remained strong and people would still be necessary even in an AI world. "My guess is we will still need a bunch of hires," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 If anyone is in doubt, he told them, just browse OpenAI's careers page. "There's like 800 job listings," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260506em560000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
