\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: After ICE Raid, a Business Rebuilds --- Philadelphia carwash was tested 'at every level' following seven workers' arrests\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Simon \par \uc2 994 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA -- Jeffrey Lee has cycled through more than 100 workers after agents arrested seven employees at his family's carwash last year. Some new hires couldn't last an hour on the job.\par \par \uc2 Masked Immigration and Customs Enforcement agents raided Complete Autowash on a chilly Tuesday morning in January 2025, eight days after President Trump's second inauguration.\par \par \uc2 The arrests of the seven workers, who ICE said were all in the U.S. illegally, dealt a major blow to \uc2 Lee\uc2 's small family business. He has spent 15 months rebuilding. "It tested me at every level. Spiritual. Mental. Physical," he said. "When bad things happen, which they do, often, it's not about what happened, but how you move forward."\par \par \uc2 Business has picked up, but not consistently enough for Complete to return to profitability. It now cleans roughly 200 to 220 cars a day, down by about 80 from before the raid. \uc2 Lee\uc2  isn't sure how much of the current downturn stems from customers staying away since the ICE action and how much reflects stretched consumers.\par \par \uc2 "We were on shaky ground for a long time," said Lee, age 37. "It's still not stable, but it's more stable than it was."\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration in the past year has conducted raids across the U.S. targeting big businesses such as Hyundai and, more often, smaller ones like Lee's.\par \par \uc2 "Worksite enforcement remains a cornerstone of our efforts to protect public safety, national security, and economic stability," ICE said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 All but two of the employees had worked at Complete for a decade or more. Lee said he assumed that the workers were in the U.S. legally. "They all presented Social Security cards and things like that," he said. "We don't dig too deep. We are just a carwash."\par \par \uc2 Lee\uc2  started learning the carwash business at age 8 or 9. He would spend weekends with relatives who owned a carwash in West Chester, Pa., rising at 5 a.m. to help his uncle open up.\par \par \uc2 Lee's parents -- who immigrated from South Korea before he was born -- purchased one of Philadelphia's oldest carwashes 19 years ago for roughly $1.5 million.\par \par \uc2 He started at Complete at age 18, working as a cashier. At the time, Lee was in a wheelchair after losing a leg and a portion of his pelvis when he was hit by a speeding car at age 14. These days he gets around on crutches.\par \par \uc2 Lee\uc2 's father opened up the business the morning of the raid. A little after 9 a.m., more than a dozen ICE agents arrived, according to worker statements included in immigration court filings. They apprehended eight workers. ICE quickly released one, a U.S. citizen whose father was taken. The others were loaded into two large vans.\par \par \uc2 When Lee arrived, ICE and the employees were gone.\par \par \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  records show that ICE acted based on information about undocumented workers at the carwash received by its tip line.\par \par \uc2 Six detained employees were from Mexico and one was from the Dominican Republic. One worker agreed to leave the country; a second was deported. Four were released on bond, and one remains in ICE detention.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Griffin, an attorney representing the employees, said in an immigration court filing that ICE acted with "neither a warrant nor probable cause," violating the Constitution and its own laws and regulations. One employee was being sponsored for legal status by his American-born son, Griffin said.\par \par \uc2 After the raid, Lee quickly took to the phone, asking former employees for help to get the carwash staffed back up. It reopened a day later with a skeleton crew of four.\par \par \uc2 The raid shook Philadelphia's immigrant community. It also brought an outpouring of job seekers. Lee cycled through about 120 people. Eighty failed. Of the 39 who passed the initial trial period, 19 remain.\par \par \uc2 New employees start wiping down cars emerging from the tunnel, then learn other roles. Twenty or more workers didn't last an hour. They moved too slow, couldn't pick up required skills, lacked a work ethic.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe I was overoptimistic about a person's skill set or ambitions," Lee said. "That was a disappointment."\par \par \uc2 Sales dropped 35% after the raid, as customers stayed away, some for months. Lee briefly suspended a membership program for unlimited washes because the service wasn't up to snuff.\par \par \uc2 "It was complete chaos," Lee said. "Our service was horrible."\par \par \uc2 Complete paid an attorney $25,000 to deal with DHS, put off plans to renovate the office and bathroom, and delayed a planned price increase.\par \par \uc2 The old crew ranged in age from about 20 to 50. The staff today is mostly in their 20s. Just two pre-raid employees remain on the job.\par \par \uc2 New hires start at $12 an hour, with raises for performance and good attendance, just as they did before the raid. Because of their seniority, the workers who were arrested were earning $15 to $18 an hour, plus tips.\par \par \uc2 Nathaniel Padua, 20, joined Complete last year after stints at fast-food restaurants. "My folks have been coming here for years and recommended me," he said. At Complete, "they were helping me learn how to work fast. They treat me like family."\par \par \uc2 As he did before the raid, Lee spends much of his day behind a plexiglass cashier's window littered with brightly colored Post-it Notes, stacks of car fresheners to one side. Additional fresheners, car mats and other supplies hang on the wall behind him.\par \par \uc2 Service has improved but remains a work in progress. "They didn't put the tire shine on?" Lee asked one customer. "I've got some new people out there, brother, so give me a moment."\par \par \uc2 "I know it's not the originals," the customer responds.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260506em560008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
