\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 PayPal\uc2  Will Eliminate 20% of Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elias Schisgall and Katherine Hamilton \par \uc2 483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PayPal\uc2  plans to cut 20% of its staff over the next two to three years, according to a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The job eliminations are part of \uc2 PayPal\uc2 's new plans to accelerate its adoption of artificial intelligence and cut costs. The planned reduction would amount to 4,760 positions based on the 23,800 employees \uc2 PayPal\uc2  reported having at the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs were reported earlier by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Enrique Lores\uc2  told investors Tuesday that he thought \uc2 PayPal\uc2  has underinvested in its technology platform and was falling behind other financial services companies. He wants to cut down on what he sees as unnecessary layers of the business to spend more on AI and becoming a technology leader.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 PayPal\uc2  needs to focus," he said. "We need to recommit to the fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 Lores, who joined in March after \uc2 Alex Chriss\uc2  was ousted, said he wanted to trim costs so the company could spend more on putting out new technology.\par \par \uc2 "First, we will remove duplication and layers from our organizational structure. Second, we will accelerate our AI adoption and automation across our operations," Lores said.\par \par \uc2 Shares fell 7.7% to close at $46.49 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The cost-cutting efforts are expected to yield, at a minimum, $1.5 billion in gross run-rate savings over the next two to three years, management said. \uc2 PayPal\uc2  didn't specify which areas of the business would see cost reductions.\par \par \uc2 This year and next, \uc2 PayPal\uc2  will be rearranging its teams and building new systems and processes to run the business, management said.\par \par \uc2 PayPal\uc2 's board brought in Lores, a former chief executive of \uc2 HP\uc2 , earlier this year because it was unhappy with "the pace of change" under Chriss. The company had been trying to diversify beyond its checkout business, which had seen growth slow since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Lores said his highest priority was the checkout business, which lets customers use \uc2 PayPal\uc2  when making purchases with online retailers. He said he saw room for growth in buy now, pay later, as customers are increasingly looking for flexible payment options.\par \par \uc2 His other focuses are financial services, Venmo and payment processing.\par \par \uc2 PayPal\uc2  reported a lower first-quarter profit of $1.11 billion, or $1.21 a share, compared with $1.29 billion, or $1.29 a share, a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Stripping out certain one-time items, adjusted earnings were $1.34 a share, ahead of the $1.27 that analysts were expecting, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 First-quarter revenue rose to $8.35 billion, from $7.79 billion in the year-ago quarter. Analysts were expecting $8.05 billion in revenue.\par \par \uc2 In the current second quarter, \uc2 PayPal\uc2  expects adjusted earnings to fall by high single-digit percentages, projecting a roughly 9% decrease. It said transaction margin dollars are expected to fall by about 3%.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260506em560000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
