\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Trump Has an IRA to Sell You\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 History teaches that once entitlements are created they keep growing on autopilot. President Trump on Thursday elated progressives by laying the ground for a new retirement entitlement for Americans who don't have access to employer-sponsored plans.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump for good reason wants more Americans to share in the rise in asset values and wealth creation. But the path to fiscal profligacy is paved with good intentions. The President's executive order directs the Treasury Secretary to establish a website called "TrumpIRA.gov" that would market low-cost individual retirement accounts from private financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 The government will essentially become a broker and quasi-sponsor of retirement accounts. There's nothing to stop Americans now from setting up and making tax deductible contributions to IRAs. The Trump order is intended to sweeten the deal for workers by offering a federal matching contribution of up to $1,000.\par \par \uc2 A 2022 law provides low-income workers with a government match of up to $1,000 -- paid in the form of a refundable tax credit directly into a worker's IRA. So a worker would get the tax credit even if he has no tax liability. Workers will have to earn less than $35,500 to be eligible for the match, though White House economic chief \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  said it will work with Congress to raise the income threshold.\par \par \uc2 One risk is that these IRAs that come with a government imprimatur -- and thus potentially a backstop -- will squeeze out other private savings options. Some businesses might prefer not to assume the costs and legal burdens of sponsoring retirement accounts when the feds will do so and throw in a $1,000.\par \par \uc2 It's true that many lower-income Americans aren't saving for retirement, but the problem has been exaggerated. About 83% of full-time workers have access to retirement benefits, as do half of employees in the bottom quartile of earnings, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Workers tend to save more for retirement as their incomes rise.\par \par \uc2 Participation by lower-income workers in employer-sponsored retirement plans has declined over the past few years even as their access to benefits has increased. The take-up rate for the lowest earners has decreased by eight percentage points since 2020. One reason may be that inflation has eroded their paychecks.\par \par \uc2 The idea of "universal retirement savings accounts" isn't new. Barack Obama in 2015 launched a program that allowed workers to contribute to a government-backed retirement account via automatic payroll deductions. The program, which cost the government $70 million, closed 17 months later after only 30,000 workers enrolled and contributed a mere $34 million.\par \par \uc2 Some 20 states require employers that don't offer retirement benefits to automatically enroll their workers in state-backed plans with automatic payroll deductions. We've criticized such plans because they expand taxpayers' liability for private worker retirements and augment political control over savings. Mr. Trump's plan could do the same.\par \par \uc2 Progressives are already calling for a bigger federal match. Don't be surprised if Democrats later seek to hijack the plans to advance their environmental, social and governance (ESG) agenda. Boosting retirement savings is a worthwhile goal, but there are better ways of doing it, such as by easing fiduciary regulations for employers that sponsor plans.\par \par \uc2 The best way to get Americans to save more for retirement is by bringing down inflation and growing real wages.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Goodbye, Government Plan" -- WSJ May 14, 2026)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260504em540005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
