\par\par \b \uc2 Actors Union Signs Deal With Studios\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 249 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The union representing actors has reached a tentative multiyear deal with the major studios and streamers on a new contract, making unlikely a repeat of the 2023 strikes that crippled Hollywood for several months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  pact comes just over a week after the Writers Guild reached a new four-year deal with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 . Like the actors, writers also struck in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The entertainment industry is in the midst of severe economic challenges and neither actors nor studios are itching for a fight.\par \par \uc2 Employment in TV and movie production has declined by 30% since the late 2022 peak. Many streamers have reduced the amount of scripted content they are producing, and work in Los Angeles in particular is shrinking.\par \par \uc2 Terms of the agreement weren't disclosed.\par \par \uc2 Some sticking points in negotiations had been the Guild's desire for increased residuals for streaming content and for more safeguards against the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2  said its National Board will review the tentative agreement in the next several days. The agreement covers scripted content including movies and television shows. The current contract had been set to expire on June 30.\par \par \uc2 The studios and streamers next will turn their attention to a new contract with the Directors Guild, which historically has been done without the labor strife that has often occurred with actors and writers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260504em540003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
