\par\par \b \uc2 KPMG\uc2  To Lay Off 4% in U.S. Advisory\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark Maurer \par \uc2 241 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  is laying off 4% of its U.S. advisory business in response to weaker demand for regulatory and other services, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The Big Four accounting firm informed staff Wednesday that it would be primarily letting go of consultants who specialize in regulatory risk advisory, customer operations and financial services, the people said. About half of the cuts were lower-performing consultants, and no partners were affected.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. advisory business has more than 10,000 people, meaning roughly 400 people were affected.\par \par \uc2 The firm has grappled with slower demand for advisory services and lower-than-expected levels of voluntary attrition following overhiring during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  said the advisory business, particularly related to areas such as transactions, strategy and artificial intelligence, is growing.\par \par \uc2 "These actions focus on a strategic realignment to make sure our people's skills and capabilities are aligned with future demand," the firm said, confirming the cuts but declining to provide the percentage of its workforce to be affected.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to support our people in upskilling for the future, while evaluating the size, shape and skills of our workforce to best serve the market," the firm added.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's deregulatory approach has hurt demand from banks and other financial-services clients for some consulting providers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mark Maurer writes for the WSJ Leadership Institute's CFO Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260430em4u0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic