\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Discusses 'Apprentice' Reboot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Toonkel and Dana Mattioli \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 First it bought the "Melania" documentary. Now \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is discussing a potential reboot of "The Apprentice," the reality TV show that once starred President Trump and propelled him to national fame.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the matter said executives at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  internally have discussed casting someone very close to Trump as the host if they were to move ahead with filming new episodes: his oldest son \uc2 Donald Trump Jr.\par \par \uc2 Discussions are still in the early stages, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  hasn't approached the Trump family, but if the show gets made, it would appear on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's Prime Video service.\par \par \uc2 President Trump hosted the show, in which contestants competed for a job working for the businessman, when it aired on NBC for 14 seasons. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  inherited the back catalog when it acquired the \uc2 MGM\uc2  film and television studio in 2022.\par \par \uc2 "Since our acquisition of \uc2 MGM\uc2 , we have had preliminary internal discussions about what's next for 'The Apprentice' as a property," said an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesman, adding that the show isn't in active development and any potential host hasn't been selected.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for the White House and Trump family didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's deliberations about rebooting the TV show -- and potentially casting Trump's son in the starring role -- is the latest example of how it has sought programming that would appeal to the president and his base of conservative supporters. In some instances, the first family has profited from the push.\par \par \uc2 Amazon MGM Studios head \uc2 Mike Hopkins\uc2  and other \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives began talking about the reboot idea internally early last year, around the time Trump was being sworn in for his second term, according to some of the people familiar with the talks.\par \par \uc2 Around the same time, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  agreed to pay $40 million to distribute "Melania," the documentary that followed the first lady during the first weeks of the second term. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's involvement in the documentary drew scrutiny from some employees within its studio business, according to people familiar with the matter. The price \uc2 Amazon\uc2  paid was nearly three times the next highest offer for the film, and The Wall Street Journal reported that Melania Trump's cut was more than 70%.\par \par \uc2 Rebooting "The Apprentice" would fit into \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's push to appeal to broader audiences as it competes with \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and others for viewers. For example, Hopkins has been pushing for more faith-based programming to differentiate Prime Video, according to former executives.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesman said the company produces hundreds of shows and films each year to serve audiences with globally diverse tastes and, like other studios, has made new investments in faith-based shows and films.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260430em4u0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
