\par\par \b \uc2 Markets: \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  Cuts 3% of U.S. Workers In Restructuring\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark Maurer \par \uc2 427 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Accounting firm \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  laid off about 3% of its U.S. workforce as part of a restructuring aimed at addressing lower-than-expected levels of voluntary attrition, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The firm informed affected staff last week that it would be letting go of roughly 250 people, or 3% of its U.S. workforce totaling more than 7,700, the people familiar with the matter said. Audit, tax and advisory personnel were affected.\par \par \uc2 "We regularly evaluate our current business demand to ensure we are positioned to deliver exceptional service to our clients while maintaining a strong talent pipeline," \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "This assessment sometimes results in targeted workforce adjustments to align our staffing levels with business needs," the company said.\par \par \uc2 The cuts come two years after Forvis acquired the U.S. unit of \uc2 Mazars\uc2  as part of a new international partnership, creating a combined global audit and advisory network with roughly $5 billion in annual revenue.\par \par \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  reported more than $5.7 billion in global revenue, up 11% from a year earlier, for the year ended August 2025.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  booked $2.2 billion for the year ended May 2025.\par \par \uc2 That ranks it 10th among U.S. accounting firms, according to an Accounting Today ranking from March.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  partnership was part of a wave of consolidation sweeping professional services.\par \par \uc2 Many accounting firms in recent years have revised their ownership structures as they faced greater capital needs and difficulties in recruiting workers.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of these firms have sold stakes to private-equity investors, while other firms have struck deals with fellow accounting firms.\par \par \uc2 Forvis was formed from a merger of \uc2 BKD\uc2  and \uc2 Dixon Hughes Goodman\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Mazars\uc2  served as the longtime accounting firm for President Trump's company, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many accounting firms have been working to undo the overhiring that they did during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Firms have experienced lower-than-expected levels of voluntary attrition for years, which has contributed to layoffs.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , for example, is cutting 10% of its U.S. audit partners after years of pushing more of them to voluntarily retire early.\par \par \uc2 Firms also face the challenge of maintaining employee morale while reorganizing their operations or making other changes.\par \par \uc2 Forvis Mazars\uc2  recently sent employees to a popular team-building event in Spain in which people stand on each others' shoulders to form human towers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mark Maurer writes for the WSJ Leadership Institute's CFO Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260428em4s0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
