\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Angst About Jobs Colors Economic Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart and Owen Tucker-Smith \par \uc2 1,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate is low. The stock market is high. Consumer spending is healthy. But ask Americans how they feel about the economy, and they sound like there's a recession going on.\par \par \uc2 That matters first because when people's perceptions of the economy sour, their behavior can change and slow the economy. And second, because sometimes they are seeing real problems that have yet to show up in the hard data.\par \par \uc2 Since the 1960s, economists have been asking Americans if they think unemployment will rise or fall in the year ahead. For a year, unemployment expectations have been at highs that in the past occurred only surrounding recessions.\par \par \uc2 In Boise, Idaho, Andrew Morstad's co-working space has been hit with a bad-luck streak: An \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  engineer was laid off, while others think they are about to be.\par \par \uc2 Morstad still has a job, but he sees warning signs flashing. "I think the economy's in for a hit soon," said Morstad, a software engineer consultant. "You take those high earners out of the economy and you're taking a big chunk of tax revenue too."\par \par \uc2 Morstad's customers have been tightening their budgets, and he has been tightening his own: Normally one to go out for food and drink two or three nights a week, he said he is reining in his social life. Despite the economy's on-paper health, Morstad predicts widespread layoffs will deal the labor market a blow in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Friday, the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  said its measure of consumer sentiment fell to 49.8 in April, its lowest level in the 70-plus years the university has been regularly polling American consumers. People's assessment of the current state of the economy and their expectations for the future fell, with declines occurring across the board regardless of political affiliation, income, age or education.\par \par \uc2 "Consumers continue to be really worried about the trajectory of inflation and over the last year there's been a pretty substantial weakening in labor market expectations," said Joanne Hsu, the survey's director.\par \par \uc2 The gloom is understandable. The Iran war has driven up gasoline prices. Whiplash tariff policies are hammering small businesses. The job market, while healthy by a lot of measures, is dotted with news of high-profile layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Still, the sentiment figure contrasts with other economic reports out this week suggesting the economy is not close to a recession.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Commerce Department reported that retail sales were solid in March, even after adjusting for elevated gasoline prices. On Thursday, the Labor Department reported that the level of jobless claims remained low. Meanwhile, the stock market is trading near record highs.\par \par \uc2 It isn't just the Michigan survey throwing off bad vibes. March readings from both the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  and the Conference Board showed consumers were in a foul mood on the economy. An April poll from the Associated Press-NORC Center for Public Affairs Research released Tuesday showed that 73% of Americans think the economy is doing poorly.\par \par \uc2 Although pump prices have been higher in the past, especially after adjusting for inflation, the size of March's jump in prices from a month earlier, in both dollar and percentage terms, was unprecedented. The pinch of higher gasoline prices is real, especially for lower-income Americans. But because gasoline is a frequent purchase, with well-advertised prices, it can play an outsize role on consumer moods.\par \par \uc2 A family feeling skittish about the future might think twice about buying a house or starting a small business. A company might pause hiring. If enough people hold back, those choices can ripple through the economy.\par \par \uc2 But people's views on the economy and their behavior don't always align. In June 2022, inflation was soaring, and the sentiment reading hit what at that point was its lowest reading on record -- but consumer spending kept growing, and the U.S. kept adding jobs at a rapid clip.\par \par \uc2 One thing that is different now, however, is that people's view of the labor market is far worse. Details of Friday's Michigan report showed that 64% of survey respondents say they think that unemployment will be higher in a year than it is now, up from 61% in March and approaching the 69% registered in November. In June 2022, that figure was just 32%.\par \par \uc2 Never before has the share of people expecting unemployment been as high as it is now without the economy experiencing a recession.\par \par \uc2 The poor marks for the job market are showing up elsewhere. Unemployment expectations in the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 's survey have also been elevated, as has the share of people judging jobs "hard to get" relative to "plentiful" in the Conference Board survey. A quarterly survey of U.S. professionals on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  shows that workers have had a downbeat assessment of the labor market for the past year.\par \par \uc2 "Workforce confidence is in the tank, and it's been in the tank for a bit of time now," said Kory Kantenga, \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 's head of economics for the Americas.\par \par \uc2 To an extent, the low readings on the job market could be a reflection of an odd equilibrium it has settled into, with very little hiring and firing, immigration restrictions curtailing the supply of workers, and uncertainty over the effects of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Despite some high-profile layoff announcements -- Nike and Meta Platforms disclosed job cuts Thursday -- the overall level of layoffs appears quite low, as measured by weekly claims figures, the Labor Department's monthly layoff figures and the notices that larger businesses are required to file in advance of closings and mass layoffs.\par \par \uc2 But hiring is extremely low, so people seeking work keep striking out. And that could be coloring perceptions of the job market for the job seekers themselves, and for their friends and family.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Stable Employment, but Higher Expenses\par \par \uc2 Rosalyn Ruiz, who works in the Ohio State University's medical-billing department, is staring down two economies. On one hand, she has a stable job in a growing city.\par \par \uc2 On the other, life has grown increasingly unaffordable for Ruiz, a single mother who has cut out her weekly fast-food meals and collected as many coupons as she can find. Her children, 20 and 24, have struggled to find work. One of them is moving back into the family home. "I'm not spending on anything frivolous," she said. "It's all food and gas and pets."\par \par \uc2 Ruiz has no expectation of losing her job, but she feels the outlook for her daughters is grim.\par \par \uc2 "It's fast food or it's packing boxes," she said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich296\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4440
