\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Offers Buyouts to 7% of Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is offering long-tenured employees voluntary buyouts, a first for the software giant as it continues to reorganize staff around its push to accelerate its artificial-intelligence efforts.\par \par \uc2 The voluntary retirement program, as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  calls it, is part of a broader shift by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  to alter its performance system and how it awards bonuses and stock options, according to a memo to employees from Chief People Officer Amy Coleman viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "Across the company, we're looking at where we can simplify to move faster and deliver the solutions our customers count on," Coleman wrote. "To sustain this pace, we have to stay focused on doing great work, trusting and empowering our managers, and simplifying to support everyone." Coleman wrote that the voluntary buyout is being offered to a small percentage of long-serving U.S. employees.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 7% of the company's U.S. employees are eligible, according to a person familiar with the matter. To be eligible, employees must be at a senior director level or below, and their years of employment and age must add up to at least 70. The company is also changing the way it awards stock, with it no longer being directly tied to bonuses.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  employed about 125,000 people in the U.S. as of June 2025.\par \par \uc2 The AI boom, and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's urgency to respond to it, have set off a cascade of changes in the past year at the 51-year-old company. After striking gold with its investment in \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has been on the defense lately. Investors have sent its shares down about 20% in the past six months due to a mix of issues, including the company's dependence on \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , its struggles to build its own AI models and products, and broader industry worry about the costs and payoffs of building out AI data centers.\par \par \uc2 In the past several months, Chief Executive \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2  has made changes to the leadership of its Copilot AI and gaming teams while also dealing with several high-profile departures.\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  named \uc2 Dan Shapero\uc2  as its new CEO of \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , succeeding \uc2 Ryan Roslansky\uc2 , who recently became part of a new group in charge of its \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  365 apps. In October, Nadella promoted \uc2 Judson Althoff\uc2  to head the company's commercial business, putting him in charge of a swath of operations.\par \par \uc2 Nadella has also made changes aimed at sorting out challenges with Copilot, its family of AI products. Copilot has struggled with problems, The Wall Street Journal reported.\par \par \uc2 In March, Nadella created a unified Copilot team led by Jacob Andreou, who headed product and growth for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  AI. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  AI Chief Executive \uc2 Mustafa Suleyman\uc2 , a splashy hire in 2024, saw his remit shrink to focus on the company's proprietary AI models.\par \par \uc2 Rajesh Jha\uc2 , who worked closely with Copilot teams and oversaw Windows products, announced his retirement in March. Another notable departure this year was \uc2 Phil Spencer\uc2 , who served as CEO of gaming.\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  laid off more than 15,000 employees.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
