\par\par \b \uc2 Meta Layoffs Take Aim At Rising AI Costs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2026\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  will lay off 10% of staff, or roughly 8,000 people, in May as it seeks to streamline its operations and pay for massive investments in artificial intelligence, the company said Thursday in an internal memo.\par \par \uc2 In the memo sent to current employees that was viewed by The Wall Street Journal, Chief People Officer \uc2 Janelle Gale\uc2  said the cuts were necessary to allow the company to operate more efficiently and offset its investments.\par \par \uc2 "This is not an easy trade-off and it will mean letting go of people who have made meaningful contributions to \uc2 Meta\uc2  during their time here," Gale wrote.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would also cancel plans to hire for 6,000 open roles, the memo said. Affected employees will be notified on May 20.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesperson declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  plans to spend as much as $135 billion on AI infrastructure this year as it seeks to build what it calls personal superintelligence for its 3.5 billion daily users. The company released its first new AI model in a year, called Muse Spark, this month, and said it has plans for more releases soon.\par \par \uc2 To train its new models, \uc2 Meta\uc2  rolled out a tool this week that will track employees' keystrokes, mouse movements and click locations to teach "the next generation of our AI models to use computers," according to another internal memo viewed by the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Several employees posted questions and complaints about the new tool on an internal discussion board. The top comment: "This makes me super uncomfortable. How do we opt out?"\par \par \uc2 In a separate memo earlier this week, \uc2 Meta\uc2  Chief Technology Officer \uc2 Andrew Bosworth\uc2  said the company is building toward a vision where agents primarily do the work. "Our role is to direct, review and help them improve," he said.\par \par \uc2 The new layoffs come on top of 1,500 jobs \uc2 Meta\uc2  already cut in January from its Reality Labs division. \uc2 Meta\uc2  did layoffs for the first time in company history in 2022 after nearly doubling its staff during the pandemic to a peak of 87,000. At the time, \uc2 Meta\uc2  faced a slumping digital-ads market and a falling stock price and cut 11,000 roles.\par \par \uc2 The following year, Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  declared 2023 \uc2 Meta\uc2 's "year of efficiency" and said the company would cut 10,000 more jobs. By year-end, \uc2 Meta\uc2 's head count had shrunk to 67,000.\par \par \uc2 In the subsequent years, the number of employees climbed again, and in early 2025, Zuckerberg announced cuts again, saying he had "decided to raise the bar on performance management and move out low performers faster." The company cut 5% of its workforce, at the time about 3,700 jobs.\par \par \uc2 As of December 2025, according to \uc2 Meta\uc2 's latest reported data, head count had reached 78,865.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020260424em4o00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
